WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.277 --> 00:00:03.870
The plant-based diet is abundant in nutrients.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.070
As long as you are choosing a large variety of fruits,

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:10.620
veggies, whole grains, beans, nuts, seeds, tofu, tempeh,

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.680
all of those incredible ingredients.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.780
You're gonna get everything that you need.

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:18.290
The health benefits that I've seen in my patients

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:20.280
in the short term and the long term,

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.180
and what we see in the data as well,

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:26.110
show us that a plant-rich and even a plant-exclusive diet

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:27.902
is brilliantly healthy.

00:00:27.902 --> 00:00:30.569
(upbeat music)

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.910
Greetings, internet. Welcome to the podcast.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:39.770
Today, we're back, talking diet, talking nutrition,

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:41.210
and overall wellbeing

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:44.240
with an additional focus on hormone health,

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.470
particularly, but not exclusively, women's hormone health.

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:51.550
And we're gonna do it with Dr. Gemma Newman.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.310
Gemma is a graduate of the University of Wales

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:55.470
College of Medicine.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.440
She has expertise in a variety of specialties,

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.560
and serves as a senior partner

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.020
at a family medicine practice in the UK.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.670
She is also the author of "The Plant Power Doctor,"

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:09.460
a primer on the power of plant-based nutrition

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:13.870
to prevent and reverse many common chronic illnesses

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.760
from diabetes and heart disease to obesity,

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.380
as well as the science that explains why it works.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:21.750
Gemma first appeared on the show

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.680
almost exactly three years ago, RRP 449.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.570
It was quite a popular episode, in case you missed it.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:31.100
And today, she's back to get granular

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.570
on the relationship between nutrition and hormones

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:39.520
with a focus, but again, not an exclusive focus on women.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.140
We talk endometriosis, fibroids, menopause,

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.770
testosterone replacement therapy,

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.120
important foods, nutrients, and supplements

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.010
to best balance our hormones, and many other topics.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.630
It's worth pointing out that I learned quite a bit

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:56.960
from this conversation.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.310
It's not just for the women out there.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.660
Plenty of valuable information for everybody.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.180
So do me a huge favor,

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:07.070
take a beat, and quickly hit that subscribe button

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:10.090
and notification bell you see right down there

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.280
in the right hand corner.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.770
Thank you very much, and enjoy this conversation

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.773
with the wise and wonderful Dr. Gemma Newman.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.050
We're here, we're doing it.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.020
Yeah.
It took a minute, didn't it?

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:22.853
It did.

