We were driving here this morning

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.850
and I thought, "Wow, this looks so British."

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.100
Yeah.
And it's so much green.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:06.950
I know.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:07.783
Really surprised me.

00:03:07.783 --> 00:03:08.616
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:08.616 --> 00:03:10.076
Well, we did it just for you Gemma.

00:03:10.076 --> 00:03:11.749
Thank you.
To I make you feel at home.

00:03:11.749 --> 00:03:12.927
I did.

00:03:12.927 --> 00:03:15.140
And I was trying to remember the last time I saw you.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:18.264
Was it at the retreat or was it when I was in London?

00:03:18.264 --> 00:03:19.097
I can't remember.

00:03:19.097 --> 00:03:20.890
I mean, everything's a blur because of COVID.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:22.410
Yeah I think the pandemic changes

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.470
and it warps time slightly.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.120
For sure.
Probably at the retreat.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.420
'Cause I saw you in London

00:03:28.356 --> 00:03:30.181
between that and the last I saw you.

00:03:30.181 --> 00:03:31.014
I can't remember the order of events,

00:03:31.014 --> 00:03:32.181
the older I get.
Yeah.

00:03:32.181 --> 00:03:33.652
I don't remember what came before what.

00:03:33.652 --> 00:03:34.485
It's okay.
But anyway,

00:03:34.485 --> 00:03:36.783
you are here, lots of stuff to talk about.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.550
And because the first time you were on the show

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:42.710
was many years ago,

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:46.360
and also because it was recorded at the retreat

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.930
in kind of a fireside format,

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:50.670
I think it would be helpful in instructive

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.990
to just sort of introduce yourself,

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:54.240
your background a little bit.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:55.990
And this journey that you've been on

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:58.380
that led you to being this advocate

00:03:58.380 --> 00:03:59.930
for the plant-based lifestyle.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:00.920
Okay.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:05.637
So I've been a doctor now for oh, 18 years,

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.270
and I always knew I wanted to be a doctor.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.420
I felt that very strongly from a young age.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:14.940
But in my medical training, I think it's fair to say,

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.840
I didn't necessarily look after myself,

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:19.040
a lot of late nights, a lot of studying.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.470
And then when I went into actually being a doctor,

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:24.150
again, there was loads of night shifts, and-

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.700
Does anyone look after themselves as a medical student

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.728
or as a young doctor?

00:04:28.728 --> 00:04:29.749
Probably not.

00:04:29.749 --> 00:04:31.436
I mean, there's really not a lot of room for that.

00:04:31.436 --> 00:04:32.436
(Gemma laughs)
Probably not.

00:04:32.436 --> 00:04:34.050
That would be an unusual outlier case, I would think.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:35.530
You're right I think it would be.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:37.470
And although there is,

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:38.940
I don't know what it's like in the US,

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.830
but in the UK there this time for sports

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:44.823
and stuff like that, sort of recreational stuff,

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:47.460
but I didn't really partake of that.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.090
I was a real bookworm.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:51.800
And yeah, I partied as well, of course,

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:54.580
but I think it's safe to say

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:57.110
that my focus was not my health then.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:04:57.943
Mm-hmm.
Like you say,

00:04:57.943 --> 00:04:59.200
most doctors at that stage

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.950
don't really focus on health too much, which is ironic.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:05.900
But I felt like when I started work,

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.280
something had to change.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:09.080
'Cause I was exhausted all the time.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.580
And I was falling asleep.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.187
I was coming home from late shifts and I thought,

00:05:14.187 --> 00:05:17.070
"I can't be doing this job for the rest of my life

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.620
whilst feeling this way."

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:19.840
So I knew something had to change.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:23.450
And at that time, I hadn't done a great deal of research.

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:26.750
I just thought, well, I will calorie count,

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:28.910
I will exercise, and I will cut the carbs

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:32.230
because that's what the background hum was telling me to do.

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:34.750
In med school, I learned about nutritional deficiencies.

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:37.520
I learned pathology, and anatomy,

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.210
and communication skills, and lots of other things,

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:43.400
but not a great deal about wellness nutrition.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.460
So fast forward, I did brilliantly with that.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:47.640
I got my energy back.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:48.590
I felt great.

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:50.060
I was proud of myself.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.480
Then I realized, to my dismay,

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:54.960
that I still had risk factors for heart disease.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.800
I checked my lipid profiles and they were still raised.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:58.840
And I thought back then

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:00.100
that there was nothing I could do about it.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:01.420
That it was just genetic.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:02.800
It was my destiny.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.810
And I'd have to cope with that later in life.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:06.040
Mm-hmm.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.630
And then fast forward a few years,

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:09.960
my husband Richard introduced me

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.470
to your book "Finding Ultra."

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:12.818
Oh yeah, that's right.

00:06:12.818 --> 00:06:13.870
I forgot about that.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:14.703
Yeah.

00:06:14.703 --> 00:06:16.390
And he was running a marathon.

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:18.800
He was doing initially quite badly.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.010
He was getting inflamed, injured.

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.080
And then he decided after reading your book

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.330
and a few others that he'd go plant-based.

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:28.338
And that was my first introduction to plant-based living.

00:06:28.338 --> 00:06:29.660
I was skeptical.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:30.890
I know we talked about this before,

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:34.050
but I watched closely with skepticism.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:35.860
He did brilliantly.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:37.440
His next marathon training run,

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.890
he did like an hour and 10 minutes faster.

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:41.690
That got me really interested.

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:42.880
Mm-hmm.
Not from an athletic

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.261
point of view.

00:06:44.261 --> 00:06:45.330
I have done a couple of marathons myself,

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:46.760
but I wouldn't call myself

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:48.210
someone that's particularly athletic

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.110
or focused on performance.

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:51.910
For me, it was about my patience.

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:55.790
So were you on board eating that way also

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:57.010
in tandem with him?

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:58.930
Or you were just observing him doing that

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:00.680
and making mental notes?
Yeah.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:01.950
Skeptical mental notes.

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:04.427
Yeah, I was observing. (laughs)

00:07:04.427 --> 00:07:05.627
Uh-huh.
I didn't actually start.

00:07:05.627 --> 00:07:06.460
So two different types

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:08.660
of meals happening in the household?

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:09.493
Yeah that's right.

00:07:09.493 --> 00:07:10.787
He was cooking for himself for a while

00:07:10.787 --> 00:07:14.250
and I was just watching and learning.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.820
And don't get me wrong.

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:17.900
I had already read a little bit

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:20.570
about the environmental side of plant-based living

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:24.850
after he introduced me to your book and a couple of others.

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:25.810
So I knew that there were

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:27.640
some definite environmental benefits

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:29.330
to plant-based nutrition,

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:32.320
but I didn't have that kick myself.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:33.840
I just didn't feel like I could do it.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.040
It seemed too difficult.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:40.040
It seemed, oh, I don't know, a bit pious and not really me.

00:07:40.077 --> 00:07:40.910
Mm-hmm.

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:43.693
I had all these preconceived ideas about what it meant.

00:07:43.693 --> 00:07:44.610
Right.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:46.410
This will mean that you're gonna end up

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:48.140
carrying a picket sign? (chuckles)

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:48.973
Yeah. (chuckles)

00:07:48.973 --> 00:07:50.376
Walking around angry.

00:07:50.376 --> 00:07:52.190
(Gemma laughs)
With Birkenstocks on.

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:54.130
Well, yeah, no, I think for me,

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:58.010
I just thought, yeah, there were certain stereotypes

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:01.490
that I had contributed, sort of thought about

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:04.330
what it meant to be plant-based, what it meant to be vegan.

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:07.533
And I felt like that wasn't my identity at that time.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:10.990
So he did brilliantly.

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:11.823
And I thought,

00:08:11.823 --> 00:08:14.490
"Well, if he can do so well just running a marathon,

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.190
what could it do for health?

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:17.430
What could it do for my patients?"

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:21.820
I was thinking about heart disease, diabetes, cancer,

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:23.620
our biggest killers in the western world.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:25.217
Could it make a difference?

00:08:25.217 --> 00:08:28.230
And I realized through many years of reading

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:29.680
that, yes it could.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:31.390
I decided to jump in.

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:32.773
I did it myself.

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:36.810
And I reaped the benefits.

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:38.360
My cholesterol went down.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:39.930
I used to get a lot of knee aches as well

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:44.240
when I was running, all through my 20s, that stopped.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:46.950
So that's my own personal experience.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:48.200
But of course it's not...

00:08:49.146 --> 00:08:51.036
That's not what I base my practice on.

00:08:51.036 --> 00:08:52.036
Mm-hmm.

00:08:52.036 --> 00:08:53.680
I have to read the research as well.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:54.513
But what I found was

00:08:54.513 --> 00:08:56.300
that it was really beneficial for my patients.

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:58.710
And that's when I felt, the magic really happened for me

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:01.470
is when I could see with my own eyes,

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:04.050
the changes that my patients had experienced.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:06.140
And that fueled my passion.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:09.000
And I've been really passionate about it ever since.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:12.350
So talk to me a little bit about the transition

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:16.420
from you experiencing firsthand, the benefits of this,

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:21.226
your blood panels, improving, et cetera, and moving towards,

00:09:21.226 --> 00:09:25.470
then advising or advocating this approach

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:26.380
with your patients.

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:27.780
'Cause those are two different things.

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:30.349
Yeah they are two different things.

00:09:30.349 --> 00:09:31.182
And I thought long and hard

00:09:31.182 --> 00:09:33.940
before I started to talk about this with my patients,

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:36.780
because I knew that there would be

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:38.063
a burden of evidence if you like.

00:09:38.063 --> 00:09:40.280
Like if I was gonna be saying something to my patients,

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:43.340
I needed to know that what I was saying was evidence-based.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:47.310
And so it took me a lot longer to have the courage really,

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:48.550
to start talking about it.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:52.020
But I think what many people don't realize

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.210
is that a lot of the data exists

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:56.290
and it has existed for a long time.

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:58.910
And it actually forms the basis of a lot of guidelines.

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:02.990
It's just that we are perhaps not consciously aware of that.

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:05.140
So the World Cancer Research Fund, for example,

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:08.330
tells us that fruits and veggies and whole grains and beans,

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:10.840
are the cornerstone of a cancer preventing diet.

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:12.180
The American College of Cardiology

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:13.470
talks about plant-based diets

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:15.123
for prevention of heart disease.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:18.640
We have the American Association of Clinical Endocrinologist

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:22.930
talk about how it's really helpful for preventing diabetes.

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:26.010
And in the UK, we've got the Royal College of GPs,

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:28.880
they've got their Green Impact Scheme.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:31.790
So there's actually a lot of sort of,

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:35.770
approval if you like, from large bodies

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:38.640
that are involved with disease and nutrition.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.530
But I think still there is that reluctance

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:44.540
in a lot of people to really sort of take it in.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:45.720
Well, I think there's a couple things.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:48.110
I mean, first of all, there's this notion

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:52.740
that many medical practitioners have that

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:59.530
lifestyle advice doesn't really land with the patient.

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:01.392
Like, "I can tell 'them to do it,

00:11:01.392 --> 00:11:02.392
but they're not gonna do it.

00:11:02.392 --> 00:11:03.870
So I just need to give them these meds.

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:06.760
And tell them to maybe not eat so much,"

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:09.580
or just some really general basic stuff.

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:11.510
Yeah.
But the reluctance

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:15.102
is from this place of low adoption rates.

00:11:15.102 --> 00:11:17.330
Yeah, I think, yeah, you're right.

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:19.840
And you have to believe

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:22.630
that your patient is capable of change, I think,

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:24.960
in order to start talking about it.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:29.428
And that's hard because nobody likes to be told what to do.

00:11:29.428 --> 00:11:32.510
And doctors like telling people what to do. (chuckles)

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:34.960
Because I think we've done a lot of training.

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:36.030
We wanna make things right.

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:37.330
We wanna help people.

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:39.500
So we feel like if only they'd listen to us,

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:40.510
then you know-
Right.

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:41.753
They would-

00:11:41.753 --> 00:11:42.586
I think people want their doctor

00:11:42.586 --> 00:11:43.960
to tell them what to do though.

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:44.793
Do you think so?

00:11:44.793 --> 00:11:47.290
I think people abdicate too much responsibility

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:49.857
to their doctor's advice in general.

00:11:49.857 --> 00:11:51.570
And I don't know what it's like in the UK,

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:54.550
but in the United States,

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:56.170
a general practitioner, like yourself,

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:58.270
only has a few minutes with each patient.

00:11:58.270 --> 00:12:00.750
And so there's not enough time

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:05.750
to go into great detail on all kinds of lifestyle advice.

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:08.940
And there's no kind of follow up or accountability

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:10.760
because those systems aren't in place,

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:12.440
it's really just diagnosed, prescribe,

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:13.610
move on to the next person.

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:15.140
And it's not the fault of the practitioner,

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:17.110
it's just the way the system is set up.

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:18.130
Yeah.

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:20.470
Do you feel that in your practice,

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:23.010
like have a little bit more bandwidth

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:25.300
to spend time with patients

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:27.560
and follow up with them, et cetera?

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:29.860
'Cause that piece is so important to all of this.

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:30.693
It is important.

00:12:30.693 --> 00:12:33.160
And it's honestly the reason I went into medicine.

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:35.580
And I think that I do have a great advantage

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:37.360
if I think about it now,

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:40.570
because working in National Health Service,

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:43.520
I have a patient list, nearly 3,000 patients.

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:45.730
It's actually a really small practice.

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:48.820
It's equivalent to one full-time doctor.

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:51.623
So 3000 people under my care.

00:12:53.070 --> 00:12:55.490
And we have only 10 minutes.

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:56.720
So yes, you're right.

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:58.590
We do have a very short amount of time.

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:00.690
It's not a great deal of time to talk about lifestyle.

00:13:00.690 --> 00:13:03.510
However, we do have the ability to bring people back.

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:06.826
We have that sense of continuity.

00:13:06.826 --> 00:13:09.490
Because it's free at the point of care.

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:11.980
People don't have to pay anything to see me.

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:13.210
And people can come and see me

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:14.183
as often as they want to.
Right.

00:13:14.183 --> 00:13:15.950
They just need to book an appointment.

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:20.010
So it's very, I'd say certainly compared to the US,

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:21.380
it's very easy to book an appointment.

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:24.540
There's still a lot of complaints, generally in society,

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:26.870
about how easy it is to get a doctor's appointment.

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:29.450
But I think if we compare our healthcare system

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:32.730
to the one here in the US, it's much easier.

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:34.720
And I'm able to offer that accountability.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:39.710
And also, that support and that feeling of care

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:42.560
that I think is really lacking perhaps

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:44.030
when you're dealing with a system

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:47.916
where it's very much drugs, prescribed, next, you know?

00:13:47.916 --> 00:13:48.996
Right.
So yeah,

00:13:48.996 --> 00:13:51.120
I do think I'd have a lot more freedom in that respect.

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:53.710
And that's what I love about it.

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:56.454
I love looking after my patients from cradle to grave.

00:13:56.454 --> 00:13:58.780
Again, it's slightly different where I work

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:00.250
because we deal with everything.

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:02.380
We deal with pediatric issues-

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:06.029
Obstetric issues, elderly care issues,

00:14:06.029 --> 00:14:09.140
everything and anything, people come to us first.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:10.648
Whereas I think here,

00:14:10.648 --> 00:14:13.530
they would more likely go to a specialist in their field.

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:14.697
It depends, yeah.

00:14:14.697 --> 00:14:16.620
Well, yeah, if you have something acute,

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:17.870
you're gonna find the specialist,

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:21.670
but for the everyday stuff, I feel like a lot of that now

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:25.910
is in these urgent care centers that are all over the place.

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:28.470
So there isn't, at least from my experience,

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:30.200
and I don't know what it's like for most people,

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:34.700
but I don't have like a good relationship with a single GP

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:38.038
that I go to see that kind of knows what's up with me.

00:14:38.038 --> 00:14:39.038
Yeah.

00:14:39.038 --> 00:14:39.871
It's just, if something flares up,

00:14:39.871 --> 00:14:43.010
I go to urgent care that needs immediate attention.

00:14:43.010 --> 00:14:43.843
Yeah.

00:14:43.843 --> 00:14:45.270
And I think we're seeing the disappearance

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:48.260
of that patient-doctor relationship

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:50.700
because of the big HMOs and all that kind of thing.

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:51.533
Yeah.

00:14:51.533 --> 00:14:52.910
I see that happening a little bit in the UK,

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:54.630
but I think we still do have

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:56.420
a lot more of that relationship.

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:57.990
And I've got patients that have been with me.

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:00.860
I've been at that practice now for 14 years.

00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:02.550
And I've seen people grow up.

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:05.540
I've seen families change.

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:07.750
Babies born.
Yeah, it's nice.

00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:09.470
People, yeah, passing away.

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:11.070
And sort of that whole cycle of life.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:11.903
I've seen it.

00:15:11.903 --> 00:15:12.736
I've been there for it.

00:15:12.736 --> 00:15:13.570
And I love it.

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:16.090
That's why I became a doctor, so.

00:15:16.090 --> 00:15:18.000
So you're gonna see a little bit of everything,

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.250
but I suspect the low hanging fruit

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:24.890
or what you see quite frequently are lifestyle ailments

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:29.890
like certainly obesity, diabetes and heart disease, right?

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:31.240
Yeah.

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:33.290
Which are kind of the bullseye problems

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:38.081
that a plant-based, plant-centric, plant-predominant diet

00:15:38.081 --> 00:15:42.140
has shown to be quite beneficial in ameliorating

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:46.440
or reducing the symptomology and the underlying causes.

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:48.910
Absolutely and you're right,

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:51.250
that's the bread and butter really of what I see.

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:53.880
Mostly high blood pressure and diabetes.

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:56.950
And certainly in terms of chronic care.

00:15:56.950 --> 00:15:59.140
But you'll be surprised, there's a lot of other things

00:15:59.140 --> 00:16:02.320
that can also be affected by plant-based nutrition,

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:04.264
which we may come onto.

00:16:04.264 --> 00:16:05.880
But I get a lot of people

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:08.139
suffering from hormonal health issues-

00:16:08.139 --> 00:16:13.139
Endometriosis, fibroids, period pains, menopausal issues.

00:16:13.796 --> 00:16:14.850
And believe it or not,

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:17.560
those things as well can be improved potentially

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:19.742
by eating more plants.

00:16:19.742 --> 00:16:22.580
And other issues, bowel disorders.

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:26.100
I get a lot of people with gastrointestinal issues as well.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:28.140
So yeah, there is quite a broad range of things

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:29.850
that can be affected.

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:33.250
And it's lovely to be able to implement that.

00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:35.200
I don't necessarily manage

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:38.087
to change the diet of everybody that comes in.

00:16:38.087 --> 00:16:38.920
Sure.

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:40.740
Because people come with their own ideas,

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:42.620
their own concerns, their own expectations.

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:45.150
But if they're open to suggestions,

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:48.040
then yeah, it can be really special.

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:49.500
Is it a situation in which,

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:50.547
now that you have this book out,

00:16:50.547 --> 00:16:51.900
"The Plant Power Doctor," right?

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:53.880
Like, "Oh, she's 'The Plant Power Doctor.'

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:56.650
Like, if I go in there, she's gonna tell me to eat this way.

00:16:56.650 --> 00:16:58.150
Like I don't wanna deal with that.

00:16:58.150 --> 00:17:00.064
I'm gonna go to this other doctor."

00:17:00.064 --> 00:17:01.064
(Gemma chuckles)

00:17:01.064 --> 00:17:01.897
I don't think I've had that yet.

00:17:01.897 --> 00:17:04.150
You see what I try and do with my patients is,

00:17:04.150 --> 00:17:05.580
if they're interested,

00:17:05.580 --> 00:17:07.700
I'll refer them to my website, which is free,

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:09.450
and there's loads of free resources on there.

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:11.480
So I don't tend to sell too much.

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:13.460
But you're in the media, you're out there.

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:15.380
People know who you are, come on.

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.280
You'd be surprised, in my town,

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:20.847
I think people don't tend to sign up to me because of that.

00:17:20.847 --> 00:17:22.750
But a few of my patients have caught on

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:25.030
to the things that I'm doing online

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:26.090
and they follow me online.

00:17:26.090 --> 00:17:27.650
So I do have to be mindful of that.

00:17:27.650 --> 00:17:29.040
Right.

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:30.410
But yeah.
Yeah, balancing,

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:31.987
the public version of yourself

00:17:31.987 --> 00:17:33.920
and the daily practitioner version.

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:35.630
Yeah, it's hard.

00:17:35.630 --> 00:17:36.550
Because you know,

00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:38.430
I think people do have certain expectations

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:39.907
of what their doctor should be,

00:17:39.907 --> 00:17:41.420
and how their doctor should be.

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:43.610
Right I don't want you going on podcasts all the time.

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:46.300
You're supposed to be like in the office,

00:17:46.300 --> 00:17:48.009
like looking at my labs.

00:17:48.009 --> 00:17:49.072
(Gemma laughs)

00:17:49.072 --> 00:17:49.905
Exactly.
Right?

00:17:50.756 --> 00:17:51.589
Exactly.

00:17:51.589 --> 00:17:52.890
But no, I think I've had mostly,

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:54.700
if not all, positive feedback.

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:56.500
And people have been really inspired.

00:17:56.500 --> 00:17:57.660
And it's been lovely actually.

00:17:57.660 --> 00:17:59.920
It's been a really nice addition to my practice.

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:02.890
And one that I'll be forever grateful for.

00:18:02.890 --> 00:18:04.558
Because that's why I wrote the book.

00:18:04.558 --> 00:18:07.910
It was about kind of widening my circle of compassion,

00:18:07.910 --> 00:18:09.020
making sure that people

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:11.830
who wouldn't have otherwise been on my list

00:18:11.830 --> 00:18:15.130
know about this information in a really accessible way.

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:17.810
You know, it's full color, and pictures,

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:20.290
and flickable, and easy to access,

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:22.040
and it's got the recipes too.

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:26.223
So it's literally, I wanted to make it dead simple.

00:18:26.223 --> 00:18:27.510
Well, you achieved that.

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:28.390
It's a beautiful book.

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:30.107
So congrats.
Thank you.

00:18:31.170 --> 00:18:33.840
You mentioned hormones a couple minutes ago,

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:35.615
and I want to get into that.

00:18:35.615 --> 00:18:38.160
People who have been longtime listeners

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:42.560
or viewers of this show, know that I've had all manner

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:45.510
of whole food plant-based advocates on the program,

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:48.710
medical professionals, including two recent appearances

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:51.400
by our mutual friend, Simon Hill.

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:53.789
And over the last nine years,

00:18:53.789 --> 00:18:56.200
I feel like I've covered most of the issues

00:18:56.200 --> 00:18:58.120
regarding this lifestyle on the role of nutrition

00:18:58.120 --> 00:18:58.953
and health generally.

00:18:58.953 --> 00:19:02.080
But one area that I admit to not having covered

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:04.120
with any particular focus or intensity

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:06.300
is women's health specifically.

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:09.360
How this differs from general health advice,

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:11.230
and the role of food and hormones.

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:14.400
So how do we find our way into this topic?

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:16.400
I mean, maybe start with some thoughts

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:19.630
about when you say women's health, like what does that mean?

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:22.200
How is that different from just human health in general?

00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:24.580
And we can get more focus from there.

00:19:24.580 --> 00:19:28.340
Yeah, so I mean, I did do extra qualifications

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:31.820
in obstetrics and gynecology, OBGYN, you'd call that here.

00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:33.670
And family planning,

00:19:33.670 --> 00:19:37.420
as part of, sort of my work as a doctor.

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:40.570
So for a while I worked in family planning as well.

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:44.610
And I think it's not that women's health

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:46.380
is over and above different

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:48.140
in terms of nutrition to men's health,

00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:49.970
but that there are various considerations

00:19:49.970 --> 00:19:54.970
that we would have physically that men don't have.

00:19:56.140 --> 00:19:59.470
Now obviously, the most common thing would be

00:19:59.470 --> 00:20:01.847
the fact that we have periods,

00:20:01.847 --> 00:20:05.880
and we are kind of bound by that sort of natural cycle

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:09.540
from the age of puberty to the age of menopause.

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:12.010
Menopause itself is a huge life transition,

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:14.350
which doesn't occur in the same way with men.

00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:16.160
I think because primarily

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:17.950
because there's less of a fluctuation

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:21.966
and a sort of steep decline in hormones

00:20:21.966 --> 00:20:23.900
in a relevant to men's health.

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:25.600
It's more of a gradual decline-

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:26.433
Yeah.
As opposed

00:20:26.433 --> 00:20:27.266
to a precipitous one.

00:20:27.266 --> 00:20:29.220
Exactly, it's much more gradual.

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:31.810
And I think that helps to ameliorate symptoms.

00:20:31.810 --> 00:20:33.300
Whereas for women,

00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:37.140
going through perimenopause up to menopause,

00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:40.080
then you can actually have wild fluctuations of estrogen.

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:42.150
It can go way up, it can go way down.

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:44.050
And that's really one of the main things

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:45.130
that's sort of responsible

00:20:45.130 --> 00:20:47.020
for a lot of the symptoms that women will get

00:20:47.020 --> 00:20:51.280
around their perimenopause, which we can, I suppose define.

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:54.300
But you know, hormones affect men too, obviously.

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:55.460
They affect everybody.

00:20:55.460 --> 00:20:57.400
There's actually over 50 different hormones

00:20:57.400 --> 00:20:59.940
and they have to work together in synergy

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:02.720
in order for us to perform at our best.

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:05.723
And it affects every aspect of our lives,

00:21:05.723 --> 00:21:09.030
not just puberty, menopause, periods, fertility,

00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:12.834
but everything, mood, energy, our skin,

00:21:12.834 --> 00:21:17.290
it affects sperm counts, all sorts of things.

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:20.600
So it's actually integral to all humans,

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:21.960
but I'd say for women,

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:26.370
there are a lot more considerations, especially around yeah,

00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:30.690
the fact that we carry children and that we have periods

00:21:30.690 --> 00:21:32.330
and that we have to go through menopause,

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:34.010
which are very distinct life phases

00:21:34.010 --> 00:21:37.670
that do require extra thought and attention, I think.

00:21:37.670 --> 00:21:41.450
And so what is the role that nutrition can play in this,

00:21:41.450 --> 00:21:44.550
and also lifestyle habits?

00:21:44.550 --> 00:21:49.353
So if we talk first about periods, perhaps,

00:21:49.353 --> 00:21:52.377
'cause that's probably one of the main things

00:21:52.377 --> 00:21:53.740
that women will focus on

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:56.573
from around about the age of 11 or 12.

00:21:58.270 --> 00:22:00.770
The quality of your period can change

00:22:00.770 --> 00:22:02.890
based on the nutrition that you have.

00:22:02.890 --> 00:22:05.690
So period are starting earlier,

00:22:05.690 --> 00:22:07.800
which I think is an interesting thing.

00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:12.450
I don't think that's just because of extra nutrition.

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:15.830
I think there are also other factors at play

00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:18.593
with regard to environmental pollutants

00:22:18.593 --> 00:22:19.510
and things like that.

00:22:19.510 --> 00:22:21.616
I think there's a lot of reasons

00:22:21.616 --> 00:22:23.612
why girls are starting their periods earlier.

00:22:23.612 --> 00:22:25.110
And that's really significant because essentially

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:29.190
that means that our growth is kind of done.

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:32.193
We don't tend to get much taller after we start our periods.

00:22:33.250 --> 00:22:37.000
And I think if you go through puberty

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:38.300
and start your periods too early,

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:39.520
it can put you at increased risk

00:22:39.520 --> 00:22:42.420
of actually things like heart disease at a younger age.

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:43.360
Oh, I never heard that.

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:46.010
Yeah, which is a really interesting thing.

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:48.330
And I think there's just a lot of extra hormone exposures

00:22:48.330 --> 00:22:49.163
in our environment

00:22:49.163 --> 00:22:52.720
that we haven't been able to consider before, so-

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:54.910
Meaning like environmental toxins

00:22:54.910 --> 00:22:58.930
toxins in skincare products, et cetera?

00:22:58.930 --> 00:23:02.050
In our foods that are dysregulating

00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:04.180
our natural hormonal cycles?

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:05.160
Yeah, exactly.

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:07.973
And things that mimic estrogens in our body.

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:11.746
Plastics, for example, phthalates, BPA,

00:23:11.746 --> 00:23:14.010
they have hormone mimicking effects,

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:16.950
which I think do affect things like fertility

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:20.630
and men's sperm counts, but also, potentially also affect

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:23.680
when young girls and women start their periods.

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:26.410
So yeah, there's a lot to consider now

00:23:26.410 --> 00:23:28.830
that we haven't really considered in the past

00:23:28.830 --> 00:23:30.623
because they didn't exist.

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:33.480
But I think in terms of practical things

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:35.270
that people can do?

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:37.870
Increased fiber is so important.

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:40.823
Main reason being that if you are constipated,

00:23:40.823 --> 00:23:43.090
you are actually potentially recycling

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:45.110
unwanted hormones through the body.

00:23:45.110 --> 00:23:47.573
Hormones that you would otherwise got rid of,

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:50.070
you are actually reabsorbing.

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:54.250
So trying to make sure that young girls and well children,

00:23:54.250 --> 00:23:56.560
and everybody in general doesn't get constipated

00:23:56.560 --> 00:24:01.018
is quite a good way of reducing our excess hormone exposure.

00:24:01.018 --> 00:24:04.063
I think it's important to mention that,

00:24:04.063 --> 00:24:07.950
our fat cells in our body are also hormonally active.

00:24:07.950 --> 00:24:11.130
They can produce estrogen.

00:24:11.130 --> 00:24:13.249
So if we have more of those,

00:24:13.249 --> 00:24:15.700
then we also have more exposures to estrogen

00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:18.260
as a result of that, which can affect men as well as women.

00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:20.830
But I think that that's a really important thing

00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:23.720
that again, is not necessarily talked about much.

00:24:23.720 --> 00:24:26.890
The fact that our fat is also hormonally active too.

00:24:26.890 --> 00:24:28.070
Yeah.

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:31.950
And yeah, so I think fiber is a really important thing.

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:33.730
It reduces our hormone exposures.

00:24:33.730 --> 00:24:36.699
It helps us to produce short chain fatty acids,

00:24:36.699 --> 00:24:39.640
which it's also a great for helping our hormone regulation,

00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:41.060
feeding our gut microbiome,

00:24:41.060 --> 00:24:43.800
which is important as well for hormone regulation,

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:45.720
which is also important for our immune system,

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:47.490
which affects our hormone regulation.

00:24:47.490 --> 00:24:49.490
So all these things are linked.

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:51.240
Wait, you're saying it's all related?

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:54.020
I have, yeah.

00:24:54.020 --> 00:24:57.598
I had Dr. B in here the other day,

00:24:57.598 --> 00:24:59.790
going deep on fiber once again.

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:00.810
Yeah, of course.

00:25:00.810 --> 00:25:01.904
Yeah.

00:25:01.904 --> 00:25:04.765
And his whole thing about, eating 30 different plants

00:25:04.765 --> 00:25:07.170
and setting that as a goal and the importance of fiber

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:08.860
for any number of things

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:12.770
and just how critical it is in this conversation.

00:25:12.770 --> 00:25:13.890
It tends to get lost.

00:25:13.890 --> 00:25:17.793
We focus on macronutrients or protein or,

00:25:19.925 --> 00:25:22.260
or omega 3s, all of which are important, of course,

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:26.840
but most people are fiber deficient.

00:25:26.840 --> 00:25:29.390
Like where's the, again, back to the low hanging fruit.

00:25:29.390 --> 00:25:32.430
What is the quickest, easiest change that you could make

00:25:32.430 --> 00:25:34.010
that could have the biggest impact?

00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:35.760
And increasing the fiber in your diet

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:37.750
seems to have just a multiplicity

00:25:37.750 --> 00:25:39.950
of downstream health benefits.

00:25:39.950 --> 00:25:40.783
It does.

00:25:40.783 --> 00:25:42.940
And there was a study.

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:46.030
I think it was actually led by Dr. Neil Bernard,

00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:49.820
looking at how this could affect people and their periods.

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:53.670
And they had a two month cohort of women,

00:25:53.670 --> 00:25:55.770
half were on a healthy plant-based diet.

00:25:55.770 --> 00:25:57.670
The other half were a control group.

00:25:57.670 --> 00:26:00.660
And they wanted to see, could it affect their periods.

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:02.010
And what they discovered was

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:05.300
that it did make a statistically significant difference

00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:09.910
in the length and that the heaviness of the women's periods.

00:26:09.910 --> 00:26:12.202
And also-
Just by increasing fiber.

00:26:12.202 --> 00:26:13.202
Yep.

00:26:13.202 --> 00:26:14.490
Wow.
Just by increasing fiber.

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:18.490
And it also showed raised SHBG,

00:26:18.490 --> 00:26:21.450
sex hormone binding globulin, which is an important protein

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:24.750
that essentially holds on to hormones until we need them.

00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:27.570
It's kind of like a storage molecule, if you like.

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:30.900
And what's interesting is it was able to increase that

00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:34.040
for the women who are consuming a healthy plant-based diet,

00:26:34.040 --> 00:26:36.630
which is important because that can help regulate their

00:26:36.630 --> 00:26:39.190
unwanted hormone exposure as well.

00:26:39.190 --> 00:26:43.550
And is that a function of just an increase in plant foods?

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:46.100
And by definition, an increase in fiber?

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:48.540
Or is there an aspect of that has to do

00:26:48.540 --> 00:26:53.540
with the removal of the meat and dairy products?

00:26:54.920 --> 00:26:56.130
It could be a bit of both.

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:58.270
So this is postulation-based.

00:26:58.270 --> 00:27:00.980
So that study didn't say specifically why,

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:03.230
but we know that fiber is so crucial

00:27:03.230 --> 00:27:06.610
for reducing, recycling of hormones, as I mentioned,

00:27:06.610 --> 00:27:09.880
but also meat and dairy does have a role to play

00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:11.750
with regard to hormone exposure itself.

00:27:11.750 --> 00:27:14.210
So when we consume dairy,

00:27:14.210 --> 00:27:18.117
we are consuming the milk from a cow who's been pregnant

00:27:18.117 --> 00:27:22.210
and obviously is lactating, which has extra hormones,

00:27:22.210 --> 00:27:25.810
bioequivalent hormones to human hormones.

00:27:25.810 --> 00:27:28.547
But also in the meat that we eat,

00:27:28.547 --> 00:27:31.330
there is some hormonal exposure there as well.

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:33.590
But also interestingly, I think,

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:35.840
is that meat is one of the main ways

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:39.040
that we get potential for microplastics exposures,

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:42.130
which are also affecting our hormones.

00:27:42.130 --> 00:27:44.950
I think in the UK phthalates are highest in fish,

00:27:44.950 --> 00:27:47.810
in the US, phthalates are highest in chicken.

00:27:47.810 --> 00:27:51.100
And there is some data to show that there was an association

00:27:51.100 --> 00:27:53.240
between increasing meat consumption

00:27:53.240 --> 00:27:56.370
and increasing endometrial symptoms.

00:28:01.243 --> 00:28:03.563
I'm not sure I even know what endometriosis is.

00:28:03.563 --> 00:28:05.603
Okay so let's do a little backtrack.

00:28:05.603 --> 00:28:08.053
Endometriosis is a condition

00:28:08.053 --> 00:28:12.193
that could affect up to about 10% of women.

00:28:12.193 --> 00:28:14.883
And it takes many years often to diagnose

00:28:14.883 --> 00:28:17.653
because the women who are suffering from endometriosis,

00:28:17.653 --> 00:28:21.293
just think that perhaps they've got really heavy periods,

00:28:21.293 --> 00:28:22.533
but it's so much more than that.

00:28:22.533 --> 00:28:27.133
So what it is where the womb lining tissue,

00:28:27.133 --> 00:28:30.593
which normally just grows in the lining of the womb

00:28:30.593 --> 00:28:33.523
and is shed once a month with your period,

00:28:33.523 --> 00:28:36.283
starts to grow in other parts of the body.

00:28:36.283 --> 00:28:37.953
We don't know why this happens.

00:28:37.953 --> 00:28:39.953
It predominantly happens in the pelvis.

00:28:39.953 --> 00:28:43.253
So you get that tissue that grows in the lining of the womb,

00:28:43.253 --> 00:28:44.243
in other parts of the pelvis,

00:28:44.243 --> 00:28:47.296
often sticking perhaps to bowel or to the bladder.

00:28:48.463 --> 00:28:50.153
And but interestingly,

00:28:50.153 --> 00:28:52.313
you can get endometrial deposits anywhere,

00:28:52.313 --> 00:28:56.253
even in the brain, very rarely, or in the lungs.

00:28:56.253 --> 00:28:59.143
And the problem is when you have your monthly bleed

00:28:59.143 --> 00:29:02.003
those cells will also bleed.

00:29:02.003 --> 00:29:04.963
And when they do, they cause irritation and inflammation.

00:29:04.963 --> 00:29:06.803
So no matter where they are in the body,

00:29:06.803 --> 00:29:07.813
they start to bleed?

00:29:07.813 --> 00:29:09.133
Yeah.

00:29:09.133 --> 00:29:09.966
Wow.
They will bleed.

00:29:09.966 --> 00:29:12.383
And it can cross the blood-brain barrier?

00:29:12.383 --> 00:29:13.233
Yeah.

00:29:13.233 --> 00:29:14.613
Very rare.

00:29:14.613 --> 00:29:17.353
But yeah, it's mostly around the pelvis or,

00:29:17.353 --> 00:29:19.553
deposits perhaps on the outside of the womb.

00:29:20.643 --> 00:29:23.243
But the problem is, that inflammation and irritation

00:29:23.243 --> 00:29:25.676
then causes a lot of pain.

00:29:25.676 --> 00:29:28.823
And it can cause parts of like the bladder

00:29:28.823 --> 00:29:30.753
or the bowel to stick together.

00:29:30.753 --> 00:29:32.353
It can affect fertility.

00:29:32.353 --> 00:29:34.484
It can cause painful sex.

00:29:34.484 --> 00:29:39.083
It's a really sort of far reaching condition.

00:29:39.083 --> 00:29:43.423
And if women are suffering from horrendous period pains,

00:29:43.423 --> 00:29:46.693
nausea, vomiting, have to take days off work,

00:29:46.693 --> 00:29:48.563
painful sex problems, getting pregnant.

00:29:48.563 --> 00:29:50.083
This is not normal.

00:29:50.083 --> 00:29:51.463
And they have to go to the doctors

00:29:51.463 --> 00:29:52.660
because many people will think,

00:29:52.660 --> 00:29:54.153
"Oh, it's just my period."

00:29:54.153 --> 00:29:59.153
But interestingly, periods can be really painful.

00:29:59.483 --> 00:30:01.193
More painful than a heart attack, actually,

00:30:01.193 --> 00:30:03.670
in some research tells us.

00:30:03.670 --> 00:30:04.670
Wow.

00:30:04.670 --> 00:30:05.653
That the pain of having a period,

00:30:05.653 --> 00:30:08.063
because it's because of the prostaglandins

00:30:08.063 --> 00:30:10.713
that are produced by those womb lining cells.

00:30:10.713 --> 00:30:13.123
So when you're having a bleed,

00:30:13.123 --> 00:30:17.423
these prostaglandins that cause contraction of the womb,

00:30:17.423 --> 00:30:20.303
that's what causes the pain and the...

00:30:20.303 --> 00:30:21.273
The cramping.

00:30:21.273 --> 00:30:23.053
The cramping.

00:30:23.053 --> 00:30:24.653
Those sort of feelings of nausea.

00:30:24.653 --> 00:30:27.073
If the prostaglandins get into the bloodstream,

00:30:27.073 --> 00:30:29.653
that's what causes you to have things like diarrhea,

00:30:29.653 --> 00:30:33.853
and vomiting, nausea, it can get really bad.

00:30:33.853 --> 00:30:37.843
And I think women, don't always sort of know

00:30:37.843 --> 00:30:39.643
how to advocate for themselves.

00:30:39.643 --> 00:30:41.423
People think, "Oh, it's just her period."

00:30:41.423 --> 00:30:45.023
Well, actually for a lot of women, they really do suffer.

00:30:45.023 --> 00:30:46.753
And especially when you have endometriosis,

00:30:46.753 --> 00:30:50.157
which is a whole other condition related to your periods,

00:30:50.157 --> 00:30:53.174
but causing so many more complications, so-

00:30:53.174 --> 00:30:56.633
So it goes undiagnosed often

00:30:56.633 --> 00:30:58.895
because people just think you're having

00:30:58.895 --> 00:31:00.703
a severe period episode

00:31:00.703 --> 00:31:04.863
or you're ignored and just consider to be normal?

00:31:04.863 --> 00:31:07.928
Yeah, so either doctors will think,

00:31:07.928 --> 00:31:09.313
"Oh, she's just having a heavy period.

00:31:09.313 --> 00:31:10.803
I'll give her some painkillers."

00:31:10.803 --> 00:31:13.213
Or she'll think, "Oh, I'm just having a heavy period.

00:31:13.213 --> 00:31:15.793
I'll just have to wait a few days later, it'll be okay."

00:31:15.793 --> 00:31:19.123
But for some women, it's completely life changing

00:31:19.123 --> 00:31:21.343
and it can have a huge impact.

00:31:21.343 --> 00:31:24.146
And as I say, up to 10% of women have this condition.

00:31:24.146 --> 00:31:25.478
And is there a test for it?

00:31:25.478 --> 00:31:28.943
And then once it's diagnosed, how is it treated?

00:31:28.943 --> 00:31:31.443
So this is why it's hard to diagnose

00:31:31.443 --> 00:31:35.073
because the main way of doing so is through a laparoscopy.

00:31:35.073 --> 00:31:37.063
So you have to have an operation essentially

00:31:37.063 --> 00:31:40.173
to see those deposits, which are usually in the pelvis.

00:31:40.173 --> 00:31:42.743
Right, which no one's gonna do unless it's so severe.

00:31:42.743 --> 00:31:43.576
Exactly.

00:31:43.576 --> 00:31:45.623
So people will try and muddle through,

00:31:45.623 --> 00:31:48.343
they might take some painkillers and hope for the best,

00:31:48.343 --> 00:31:51.013
but actually it's really important to get that diagnosis

00:31:51.013 --> 00:31:53.923
because once you have a diagnosis,

00:31:53.923 --> 00:31:55.503
one of the main ways of treating it

00:31:55.503 --> 00:31:58.663
is to actually kind of blast those little deposits,

00:31:58.663 --> 00:31:59.903
burn them off,

00:31:59.903 --> 00:32:02.994
and that can help to improve fertility and pain.

00:32:02.994 --> 00:32:07.603
But I would love for women to know that diet and lifestyle

00:32:07.603 --> 00:32:09.893
can have an effect on endometriosis as well,

00:32:09.893 --> 00:32:11.403
a positive effect.

00:32:11.403 --> 00:32:13.903
And having a healthy plant-rich diet

00:32:13.903 --> 00:32:17.413
could improve symptoms greatly.

00:32:17.413 --> 00:32:19.993
So yeah, I would love for,

00:32:19.993 --> 00:32:22.603
if there's one message for this part of the podcast,

00:32:22.603 --> 00:32:25.203
it would be for women who have endometriosis to know

00:32:26.263 --> 00:32:27.743
that hopefully if they have a more plant-based diet,

00:32:27.743 --> 00:32:30.393
they could see some improvement in their symptoms.

00:32:30.393 --> 00:32:31.571
Interesting.

00:32:31.571 --> 00:32:34.313
But they don't quite know what causes it,

00:32:34.313 --> 00:32:38.463
and there isn't any kind of like simplistic cure?

00:32:38.463 --> 00:32:41.043
No, there's no simplistic cure,

00:32:41.043 --> 00:32:45.063
but I suspect that our extra hormone exposures

00:32:45.063 --> 00:32:46.856
in the modern world play part,

00:32:47.883 --> 00:32:50.213
just because, as I said, there's an association there

00:32:50.213 --> 00:32:53.163
between the consumption of certain animal products

00:32:53.163 --> 00:32:56.463
and the development of further endometrial deposits.

00:32:56.463 --> 00:32:57.673
And we don't yet know,

00:32:57.673 --> 00:32:58.756
I'd love for there to be more research.

00:32:58.756 --> 00:33:00.263
It's the same with fibroids.

00:33:00.263 --> 00:33:03.053
I would love there to be more research into fibroids.

00:33:03.053 --> 00:33:04.183
Well, let's talk about that.

00:33:04.183 --> 00:33:05.803
Define what fibroids are.

00:33:05.803 --> 00:33:07.173
Okay.

00:33:07.173 --> 00:33:11.119
So fibroids are essentially growths in the womb

00:33:11.119 --> 00:33:13.353
and they are estrogen-independent.

00:33:13.353 --> 00:33:15.073
So much like endometriosis,

00:33:15.073 --> 00:33:19.563
which is in a way, fueled by estrogen exposures.

00:33:19.563 --> 00:33:23.370
So is the development of fibroids.

00:33:23.370 --> 00:33:28.013
And you can get extra painful periods,

00:33:28.013 --> 00:33:30.513
again, with fibroids without realizing it.

00:33:30.513 --> 00:33:32.843
So you'd be thinking, oh, I've just got a heavy period.

00:33:32.843 --> 00:33:35.633
But actually the reason, that you've got it on this occasion

00:33:35.633 --> 00:33:39.403
is because your fibroid mass, the lump,

00:33:39.403 --> 00:33:41.413
is pushing against the womb lining,

00:33:41.413 --> 00:33:44.213
which is causing it to increase in the surface area.

00:33:44.213 --> 00:33:47.373
Then it bleeds more, than the period is heavier.

00:33:47.373 --> 00:33:51.163
So not all fibroid lumps push into the womb lining.

00:33:51.163 --> 00:33:53.253
Sometimes they can grow on the outside of the womb,

00:33:53.253 --> 00:33:55.303
or in the middle of the womb,

00:33:55.303 --> 00:33:57.319
but for the ones that push onto the womb lining,

00:33:57.319 --> 00:33:59.703
they cause extra heavy bleeds.

00:33:59.703 --> 00:34:01.493
And if the fibroids get big,

00:34:01.493 --> 00:34:04.733
then they also have just a mass effect in the pelvis,

00:34:04.733 --> 00:34:07.333
which can affect the way that you pee,

00:34:07.333 --> 00:34:09.613
or the way that you're able to pass motions.

00:34:09.613 --> 00:34:12.533
So it can have other downstream effects as well.

00:34:12.533 --> 00:34:15.423
And again, I would really love to see more research

00:34:15.423 --> 00:34:18.013
into the causation and amelioration of fibroids,

00:34:18.013 --> 00:34:20.423
but suffice to say, in terms of lifestyle,

00:34:20.423 --> 00:34:22.453
there are some things that we've seen

00:34:22.453 --> 00:34:24.552
could be associated with it.

00:34:24.552 --> 00:34:27.163
Again, we come back to the meat consumption

00:34:27.163 --> 00:34:30.553
that can have an impact on the growth of fibroids,

00:34:30.553 --> 00:34:32.963
perhaps because of the extra estrogens

00:34:32.963 --> 00:34:35.783
or plastics that we're exposed too.

00:34:35.783 --> 00:34:37.643
Alcohol is another one

00:34:37.643 --> 00:34:40.744
that has been associated with fibroids.

00:34:40.744 --> 00:34:43.673
Physical exercise has been something

00:34:43.673 --> 00:34:45.373
that's potentially been associated

00:34:45.373 --> 00:34:47.383
with less severe fibroids,

00:34:47.383 --> 00:34:49.943
but we just have association data really.

00:34:49.943 --> 00:34:52.663
So I'd like to see more of it.

00:34:52.663 --> 00:34:55.483
And is there a test to know whether you have fibroids

00:34:55.483 --> 00:34:57.903
or if you have them, you just know it?

00:34:57.903 --> 00:34:59.423
You don't just know it unfortunately,

00:34:59.423 --> 00:35:00.533
but you can get a scan.

00:35:00.533 --> 00:35:02.263
It's much easier to diagnose fibroids-

00:35:02.263 --> 00:35:03.303
because you can just,

00:35:03.303 --> 00:35:05.433
you can arrange for an ultrasound scan,

00:35:05.433 --> 00:35:07.243
and you'd be able to see those lumps there.

00:35:07.243 --> 00:35:08.753
Sometimes with a scan,

00:35:08.753 --> 00:35:11.883
you can see what could be endometrial deposits.

00:35:11.883 --> 00:35:15.777
So if you have an unusual appearance to your ovaries,

00:35:15.777 --> 00:35:18.383
it could be something called a chocolate cyst

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:22.083
because you get sort of dried old blood

00:35:22.083 --> 00:35:24.363
then on the ovary itself,

00:35:24.363 --> 00:35:27.023
which looks like sort of a dark cyst.

00:35:27.023 --> 00:35:27.953
And you can sometimes see.

00:35:27.953 --> 00:35:29.823
Because those cells have bled.

00:35:29.823 --> 00:35:30.656
Because they've bled-

00:35:30.656 --> 00:35:31.929
During period.

00:35:31.929 --> 00:35:32.929
Yeah.
Wow.

00:35:32.929 --> 00:35:34.103
Exactly.

00:35:34.103 --> 00:35:37.023
So you can kind of get a hint of endometriosis

00:35:37.023 --> 00:35:38.346
through a physical exam.

00:35:40.563 --> 00:35:41.853
If your primary care physician

00:35:41.853 --> 00:35:44.863
or your OB/GYN has done an internal exam,

00:35:44.863 --> 00:35:46.063
you can sometimes get a feel

00:35:46.063 --> 00:35:47.583
for whether the endometriosis there

00:35:47.583 --> 00:35:51.563
based on whether the womb or the ovaries

00:35:51.563 --> 00:35:54.273
feel fixed when you do an internal exam.

00:35:54.273 --> 00:35:55.106
And you can again,

00:35:55.106 --> 00:35:56.743
maybe get a hint of it with an ultrasound,

00:35:56.743 --> 00:35:59.363
but the only way of truly diagnosing endometriosis

00:35:59.363 --> 00:36:00.893
is with a laparoscopy.

00:36:00.893 --> 00:36:02.943
But fibroids is much easier

00:36:02.943 --> 00:36:05.183
because you only really need to scan.

00:36:05.183 --> 00:36:08.153
And the appropriate lifestyle interventions

00:36:08.153 --> 00:36:10.298
for fibroids look like what?

00:36:10.298 --> 00:36:14.517
Well, I'd say really focus on whole foods,

00:36:14.517 --> 00:36:17.905
healthy plant-rich diet, number one.

00:36:17.905 --> 00:36:19.803
You'll be increasing your fiber.

00:36:19.803 --> 00:36:21.053
So as I said before,

00:36:21.053 --> 00:36:24.323
reducing your hormone exposure from your own body.

00:36:24.323 --> 00:36:25.853
You'll be hopefully helping yourself

00:36:25.853 --> 00:36:29.629
to potentially reduce any excess weight.

00:36:29.629 --> 00:36:32.735
And you'll be avoiding xenoestrogens

00:36:32.735 --> 00:36:35.923
from plastics exposures as well.

00:36:35.923 --> 00:36:38.513
So that would be the main thing, I'd say.

00:36:38.513 --> 00:36:40.040
Exercise is important.

00:36:40.040 --> 00:36:43.733
Interestingly, there's some research to show

00:36:43.733 --> 00:36:44.943
that certain things are great

00:36:44.943 --> 00:36:46.633
for reducing the pain of periods,

00:36:46.633 --> 00:36:49.463
which can affect endometriosis and fibroids.

00:36:49.463 --> 00:36:51.893
So ginger and turmeric

00:36:51.893 --> 00:36:53.343
have been shown in a couple of studies

00:36:53.343 --> 00:36:55.723
to be really helpful in reducing

00:36:55.723 --> 00:36:59.303
the amount of prostaglandins that are being produced

00:36:59.303 --> 00:37:01.103
at the lining of the womb.

00:37:01.103 --> 00:37:04.553
So I mean, essentially, if we kind of figure out,

00:37:04.553 --> 00:37:08.235
this is why oral contraception could help periods actually,

00:37:08.235 --> 00:37:11.588
because oral contraceptive pills reduce the amount

00:37:11.588 --> 00:37:15.280
of endometrial lining cells that are produced,

00:37:15.280 --> 00:37:17.913
which then reduces the prostaglandins,

00:37:17.913 --> 00:37:20.436
which is what causes the pain and the cramping.

00:37:22.316 --> 00:37:25.383
So yeah, so contraceptive pills can help with the pain.

00:37:25.383 --> 00:37:27.233
Antiinflammatory painkillers can help with the pain

00:37:27.233 --> 00:37:29.833
for the same reason, they reduce the prostaglandins.

00:37:29.833 --> 00:37:34.813
But if we wanted to go sort of down a lifestyle route,

00:37:34.813 --> 00:37:38.633
then ginger and turmeric have been shown in studies

00:37:38.633 --> 00:37:42.463
to have the same benefits as anti-inflammatory painkillers

00:37:42.463 --> 00:37:43.983
for reducing period pains.

00:37:43.983 --> 00:37:48.293
So on some level, this is a disease of inflammation,

00:37:48.293 --> 00:37:52.833
or a disease that's exacerbated by exposing the body

00:37:52.833 --> 00:37:57.833
to inflammatory foods and environmental factors, right?

00:37:59.352 --> 00:38:00.352
Yeah.

00:38:00.352 --> 00:38:01.313
So it would follow logically

00:38:01.313 --> 00:38:05.453
then that a diet that is anti-inflammatory in nature,

00:38:05.453 --> 00:38:07.723
I mean, it's good for everything, right?

00:38:07.723 --> 00:38:11.803
Would be particularly good at ameliorating those symptoms

00:38:11.803 --> 00:38:12.843
of these conditions.

00:38:12.843 --> 00:38:13.973
Yes, I think so.

00:38:13.973 --> 00:38:17.753
And if we look at other hormonally driven things,

00:38:17.753 --> 00:38:20.343
so not just endometriosis and fibroids,

00:38:20.343 --> 00:38:22.313
but also polycystic ovaries,

00:38:22.313 --> 00:38:26.043
again, it's another condition that affects fertility,

00:38:26.043 --> 00:38:27.678
affects our periods,

00:38:27.678 --> 00:38:32.123
but can also be improved using lifestyle.

00:38:32.123 --> 00:38:34.053
Again, it's not something that tends to come up

00:38:34.053 --> 00:38:35.573
in these kinds of conversations,

00:38:35.573 --> 00:38:38.423
but PCOS, polycystic ovarian syndrome,

00:38:38.423 --> 00:38:40.833
is very much like diabetes

00:38:40.833 --> 00:38:43.803
in that it's driven by insulin resistance.

00:38:43.803 --> 00:38:47.003
And insulin resistance can be improved

00:38:47.003 --> 00:38:48.973
by using whole plant foods,

00:38:48.973 --> 00:38:51.713
eating a much more whole few plant-based diet.

00:38:51.713 --> 00:38:54.193
And so, yeah, that's another condition

00:38:54.193 --> 00:38:57.783
that could potentially also be improved using lifestyle,

00:38:57.783 --> 00:38:59.953
and have long reaching benefits.

00:38:59.953 --> 00:39:00.786
Sure.

00:39:00.786 --> 00:39:04.373
So increasing your fiber, turmeric, ginger,

00:39:04.373 --> 00:39:07.793
what are some of the other anti-inflammatory foods

00:39:07.793 --> 00:39:09.483
that top your list?

00:39:09.483 --> 00:39:14.483
Well, we've gotta go for like berries, fruits, vegetables,

00:39:14.993 --> 00:39:17.553
nuts, and seeds, tofu, tempeh.

00:39:17.553 --> 00:39:21.713
I think soybeans have a terrible reputation still,

00:39:21.713 --> 00:39:23.573
unfortunately, but they are really good

00:39:23.573 --> 00:39:25.036
for hormone regulation.

00:39:26.153 --> 00:39:27.893
Because not only do they contain

00:39:27.893 --> 00:39:31.653
all essentially amino acids, but they also,

00:39:31.653 --> 00:39:34.843
they contain a special kind of phytoestrogen,

00:39:34.843 --> 00:39:36.863
which is actually beneficial.

00:39:36.863 --> 00:39:41.196
So it's actually more of a modulator

00:39:41.196 --> 00:39:44.623
rather than something that is an active

00:39:44.623 --> 00:39:46.503
pusher of estrogen, if you like.

00:39:46.503 --> 00:39:48.693
Right, so let's like really underscore that.

00:39:48.693 --> 00:39:52.013
Because I think what happens is people hear soy,

00:39:52.013 --> 00:39:53.363
they think estrogen,

00:39:53.363 --> 00:39:56.853
you spoke earlier about dysregulated estrogen

00:39:56.853 --> 00:39:58.523
causing these problems.

00:39:58.523 --> 00:40:00.773
So without further inquiry,

00:40:00.773 --> 00:40:03.873
it would appear that eating something like soy

00:40:03.873 --> 00:40:07.163
with these estrogen compounds

00:40:07.163 --> 00:40:09.003
would be not a good thing, right?

00:40:09.003 --> 00:40:13.333
So like, I want you to be like really clear about soy,

00:40:13.333 --> 00:40:15.698
estrogens, phytoestrogens the differences.

00:40:15.698 --> 00:40:17.933
Okay. (chuckles)

00:40:17.933 --> 00:40:21.613
soy contains phytoestrogens or estrogens,

00:40:21.613 --> 00:40:23.570
but it's actually what we call a SER,

00:40:24.551 --> 00:40:25.993
a selective estrogen receptor.

00:40:25.993 --> 00:40:28.033
Right and if you're British, you say estrogen.

00:40:28.033 --> 00:40:28.866
Yes sorry.

00:40:28.866 --> 00:40:31.041
Keep going sorry to interrupt you.

00:40:31.041 --> 00:40:32.041
(Gemma laughs)

00:40:32.041 --> 00:40:32.874
My estrogen.

00:40:32.874 --> 00:40:34.446
So it's selective in its action.

00:40:37.663 --> 00:40:38.823
So what it does is,

00:40:38.823 --> 00:40:41.303
if you are wanting to reduce your risk of breast cancer,

00:40:41.303 --> 00:40:44.395
it's really great for that because it blocks the receptors

00:40:44.395 --> 00:40:48.243
that are responsible for breast cancer.

00:40:48.243 --> 00:40:49.703
If you want to improve your bone health,

00:40:49.703 --> 00:40:50.863
it's actually really good for that

00:40:50.863 --> 00:40:53.843
because it's more likely to selectively bind

00:40:53.843 --> 00:40:57.505
to the receptors that are in the bone area of the body.

00:40:57.505 --> 00:41:01.703
So it has selective effects, which are actually beneficial

00:41:01.703 --> 00:41:03.623
in terms of our hormone exposures.

00:41:03.623 --> 00:41:07.893
It stops us from being exposed to bioequivalent hormones,

00:41:07.893 --> 00:41:09.363
and instead, blocks those.

00:41:09.363 --> 00:41:12.583
So it's good for reducing risk of breast cancer.

00:41:12.583 --> 00:41:16.133
Especially if you start to eat soy products young in life.

00:41:16.133 --> 00:41:20.483
So for young girls, teenagers, young women,

00:41:20.483 --> 00:41:21.913
there is some evidence to show

00:41:21.913 --> 00:41:25.763
that if you have soy products in childhood and adolescents,

00:41:25.763 --> 00:41:26.603
it's much more likely

00:41:26.603 --> 00:41:29.403
to reduce your risk of breast cancer later in life.

00:41:29.403 --> 00:41:31.466
But if you're a dude or a mate,

00:41:32.663 --> 00:41:35.051
and you get on the soy early,

00:41:35.051 --> 00:41:38.653
you become a soy boy and develop man boobs?

00:41:38.653 --> 00:41:40.503
Well, you're probably more like to develop man boobs

00:41:40.503 --> 00:41:43.263
if you drink actual cows milk, if you think about it,

00:41:43.263 --> 00:41:44.853
'cause that's a bioequivalent estrogen,

00:41:44.853 --> 00:41:47.343
but an actual fact there is data to show

00:41:47.343 --> 00:41:50.556
that soy can potentially reduce the risk of prostate cancer.

00:41:50.556 --> 00:41:51.389
(Rich gasps)

00:41:51.389 --> 00:41:53.429
So you don't need to worry too much about soy.

00:41:53.429 --> 00:41:54.429
All right.

00:41:54.429 --> 00:41:56.023
Or in fact, you don't need to worry at all about soy.

00:41:56.023 --> 00:42:01.023
Is there a difference between soy or tofu

00:42:01.183 --> 00:42:05.369
versus like tempeh or a fermented soy?

00:42:05.369 --> 00:42:09.483
I've always understood that a fermented version is better.

00:42:09.483 --> 00:42:13.636
Yeah, I think there are different qualities, I suppose.

00:42:15.073 --> 00:42:18.013
Interestingly, soy products have been shown

00:42:18.013 --> 00:42:22.563
to be beneficial for flushes, sort of period flushes,

00:42:22.563 --> 00:42:24.466
vasomotor symptoms in women.

00:42:25.643 --> 00:42:29.082
So yeah, but in sort of Japanese populations,

00:42:29.082 --> 00:42:31.403
they don't really have the same amount of flushing

00:42:31.403 --> 00:42:33.033
and it's thought to be because they have a lot

00:42:33.033 --> 00:42:37.583
of fermented soy products sort of meso and tempeh

00:42:37.583 --> 00:42:39.873
and edamame beans.

00:42:39.873 --> 00:42:41.513
And not that that's fermented,

00:42:41.513 --> 00:42:44.083
but it's a more natural form of soy.

00:42:44.083 --> 00:42:44.916
Yeah.

00:42:44.916 --> 00:42:45.749
But yeah,

00:42:45.749 --> 00:42:49.843
I think if you go for minimally processed sort of tofu,

00:42:49.843 --> 00:42:54.323
tempeh, edamame beans, meso soup, that kind of thing.

00:42:54.323 --> 00:42:57.753
Are you gonna have perhaps even better benefits, but yeah.

00:42:57.753 --> 00:42:58.933
What about natto?

00:42:58.933 --> 00:42:59.883
Have you had natto?

00:42:59.883 --> 00:43:02.213
I've not had it, but I've heard it's disgusting.

00:43:02.213 --> 00:43:03.513
I've heard that as well.

00:43:03.513 --> 00:43:06.723
It's a top the list though, of like the most nutritious-

00:43:06.723 --> 00:43:08.283
Yeah.
Fermented soy product.

00:43:08.283 --> 00:43:11.153
And it's an acquired taste that they enjoy in Japan,

00:43:11.153 --> 00:43:14.287
but is not necessarily readily available in the west.

00:43:14.287 --> 00:43:15.273
No, I mean, yeah.

00:43:15.273 --> 00:43:17.933
Maybe I'll try it one day, but I haven't tried it yet.

00:43:17.933 --> 00:43:22.209
It's sort of the soy version of durian, right?

00:43:22.209 --> 00:43:24.603
Like, but actually durian's delicious.

00:43:24.603 --> 00:43:25.436
Yeah.

00:43:25.436 --> 00:43:26.483
But it's an acquired...

00:43:26.483 --> 00:43:28.283
I mean durian's a hardcore thing.

00:43:28.283 --> 00:43:29.116
Yeah.

00:43:29.116 --> 00:43:30.943
I mean the people that love it really love it.

00:43:30.943 --> 00:43:32.113
Yeah.

00:43:32.113 --> 00:43:33.313
It smells quite strong though, doesn't it?

00:43:33.313 --> 00:43:34.313
Yeah, for sure.

00:43:34.313 --> 00:43:37.073
But I actually wanted to say, before we get off the topic,

00:43:37.073 --> 00:43:41.373
that interestingly, going back to The Gut Health MD

00:43:41.373 --> 00:43:44.903
and his passion for the gut microbiome,

00:43:44.903 --> 00:43:48.933
the effect of soy on flushes and things like that

00:43:48.933 --> 00:43:52.493
can be affected by our microbiome or estrobolome.

00:43:52.493 --> 00:43:55.673
So some people, unless we have the gut bugs

00:43:55.673 --> 00:44:00.673
to sort of process the genestine from the isoflavone in soy,

00:44:00.733 --> 00:44:03.093
we may not necessarily get the same flushing benefits,

00:44:03.093 --> 00:44:04.073
which is why it's great

00:44:04.073 --> 00:44:06.443
then to have an overall plant-rich diet,

00:44:06.443 --> 00:44:07.753
because what you're doing there,

00:44:07.753 --> 00:44:10.023
is you're promoting the kind of gut bugs

00:44:10.023 --> 00:44:13.053
which would then hopefully be able to metabolize

00:44:13.053 --> 00:44:15.733
the components of soy that benefit us

00:44:15.733 --> 00:44:17.333
in terms of vasomotor flushing.

00:44:17.333 --> 00:44:20.423
So if you have some form of dysbiosis,

00:44:20.423 --> 00:44:22.533
that could be a causal factor

00:44:22.533 --> 00:44:27.533
in a dysregulated hormonal state

00:44:28.033 --> 00:44:30.843
and also a dysregulated period, right?

00:44:30.843 --> 00:44:33.533
Or some kind of period issue.

00:44:33.533 --> 00:44:34.523
Yeah, potentially.

00:44:34.523 --> 00:44:36.793
So yeah, I'd say the bottom line is

00:44:36.793 --> 00:44:41.063
that plants are always good in terms of hormone regulation.

00:44:41.063 --> 00:44:43.056
And they can help to foster the microbiome

00:44:43.056 --> 00:44:46.615
that could potentially then also use other parts of plants

00:44:46.615 --> 00:44:48.206
to then benefit us.

00:44:49.518 --> 00:44:52.483
When you're advocating a plant-based lifestyle,

00:44:52.483 --> 00:44:54.413
how do you think about the difference

00:44:54.413 --> 00:44:57.023
between a plant exclusive-diet

00:44:57.023 --> 00:44:59.153
versus a plant-predominant diet?

00:44:59.153 --> 00:45:01.183
As somebody who's seeing a lot of patients,

00:45:01.183 --> 00:45:04.696
and for many, I suspect this is like a new idea.

00:45:05.813 --> 00:45:08.271
How do you communicate around that?

00:45:08.271 --> 00:45:09.803
And what is your kind of general position on that?

00:45:09.803 --> 00:45:11.400
Yeah, my general position is I just want

00:45:11.400 --> 00:45:13.413
people to eat more plants

00:45:13.413 --> 00:45:16.213
and I don't tell my patients to go vegan

00:45:16.213 --> 00:45:18.063
because I think we do have a lot of data

00:45:18.063 --> 00:45:20.513
that you can have a very healthy plant rich diet

00:45:20.513 --> 00:45:23.493
without necessarily going 100% vegan.

00:45:23.493 --> 00:45:26.223
I am somebody that identifies as vegan,

00:45:26.223 --> 00:45:27.613
but it's not something that I would say

00:45:27.613 --> 00:45:28.986
my patients have to do.

00:45:30.193 --> 00:45:32.853
I think that from what I've seen,

00:45:32.853 --> 00:45:34.903
the more plants the better,

00:45:34.903 --> 00:45:36.180
and we see that in the data as well.

00:45:36.180 --> 00:45:38.133
The more plants we eat the better.

00:45:38.133 --> 00:45:41.423
So it doesn't have to be plant exclusive,

00:45:41.423 --> 00:45:43.873
but plant predominant is definitely

00:45:43.873 --> 00:45:46.233
important for overall health.

00:45:46.233 --> 00:45:49.963
And I just, you mentioned sort of low hanging fruit earlier.

00:45:49.963 --> 00:45:51.876
That's kind of how I feel about it.

00:45:52.979 --> 00:45:55.393
Right now, only 8% of what most of people

00:45:55.393 --> 00:45:57.793
in the Western world eat is fruits and veggies.

00:45:57.793 --> 00:45:59.993
It's more than 50% processed foods.

00:45:59.993 --> 00:46:03.583
So if I can get people more on those fresh whole plant foods

00:46:03.583 --> 00:46:06.343
and less on the processed foods, then that's a win.

00:46:06.343 --> 00:46:08.553
So yeah, that's how I tend to advocate

00:46:08.553 --> 00:46:11.083
for it at work with my patients,

00:46:11.083 --> 00:46:13.363
because not, everybody's gonna feel 100%

00:46:13.363 --> 00:46:14.773
ready to just dive in.

00:46:14.773 --> 00:46:16.157
Yeah.
So yeah.

00:46:16.157 --> 00:46:18.393
Have you had any patients come to you

00:46:18.393 --> 00:46:20.503
who are on the carnivore diet?

00:46:20.503 --> 00:46:22.673
I haven't, no.

00:46:22.673 --> 00:46:23.623
Is that a thing in the UK?

00:46:23.623 --> 00:46:26.123
Cause it's like quite a thing here in the states.

00:46:26.123 --> 00:46:27.793
I know it's a big thing here

00:46:27.793 --> 00:46:30.003
and it's getting more popular in the UK,

00:46:30.003 --> 00:46:33.753
but if I'm honest, I've only really seen it in online

00:46:33.753 --> 00:46:36.873
and in wellness circles rather than in my actual patients.

00:46:36.873 --> 00:46:38.063
I think instinctively,

00:46:38.063 --> 00:46:42.803
most people feel that having a meat exclusive diet

00:46:42.803 --> 00:46:45.933
could cause long term issues. (laughs)

00:46:45.933 --> 00:46:46.993
So.

00:46:46.993 --> 00:46:48.089
How dare you?

00:46:48.089 --> 00:46:49.803
I'm sorry to say, no, but I think, yeah,

00:46:49.803 --> 00:46:50.753
it's not been something

00:46:50.753 --> 00:46:52.583
that I've actually experienced in my patients,

00:46:52.583 --> 00:46:56.293
but it's a tough one because in my practice,

00:46:56.293 --> 00:46:58.313
I never like to say to people

00:46:58.313 --> 00:47:01.293
that they're doing something that's wrong

00:47:01.293 --> 00:47:04.093
because we all have different paths to health.

00:47:04.093 --> 00:47:08.813
Having said that, it's really hard because

00:47:08.813 --> 00:47:11.203
people sometimes feel great on a carnival diet to start

00:47:11.203 --> 00:47:13.913
with they may have got rid of a lot of health issues

00:47:13.913 --> 00:47:15.503
that they were having in the past.

00:47:15.503 --> 00:47:18.413
And for me, I think that probably comes down

00:47:18.413 --> 00:47:21.793
to a fairly strict exclusion diet.

00:47:21.793 --> 00:47:23.593
You basically taking everything out,

00:47:24.533 --> 00:47:26.083
which is fine for short term,

00:47:26.083 --> 00:47:28.253
but it's not really something that you could

00:47:28.253 --> 00:47:30.313
consider long term health.

00:47:30.313 --> 00:47:32.583
Yeah that argument gets used against

00:47:32.583 --> 00:47:34.463
the plant-based diet as well.

00:47:34.463 --> 00:47:35.793
Like, oh, you feel better

00:47:35.793 --> 00:47:37.553
when you went plant-based because you just

00:47:37.553 --> 00:47:40.513
got off all the processed foods, but it's not sustainable.

00:47:40.513 --> 00:47:42.463
And over time you're gonna be

00:47:42.463 --> 00:47:44.553
nutrient mineral and vitamin deficient.

00:47:44.553 --> 00:47:48.365
It's a shame that people say that about plant-based diets

00:47:48.365 --> 00:47:51.433
because the way I see it based on the research is that

00:47:52.443 --> 00:47:55.738
the plant-based diet is abundant in nutrients.

00:47:55.738 --> 00:47:59.463
As long as you are choosing a large variety of fruits,

00:47:59.463 --> 00:48:02.553
veggies, whole grains, beans, nuts, seeds, tofu,

00:48:02.553 --> 00:48:05.083
tempe all of those incredible ingredients.

00:48:05.083 --> 00:48:09.831
You're gonna get everything that you need except the B12.

00:48:09.831 --> 00:48:13.423
But again, I think the health benefits that,

00:48:13.423 --> 00:48:15.253
I've seen in my patients in the short term

00:48:15.253 --> 00:48:18.363
and the long term and what we see in the data as well,

00:48:18.363 --> 00:48:20.623
show us that a plant rich

00:48:20.623 --> 00:48:24.353
and even a plant exclusive diet is brilliantly helpful.

00:48:24.353 --> 00:48:25.293
What about the argument?

00:48:25.293 --> 00:48:27.593
I'm sure you've seen this at least online

00:48:27.593 --> 00:48:31.073
that plants are toxic, that they have toxins

00:48:31.073 --> 00:48:33.643
that they've developed as natural defense mechanisms

00:48:33.643 --> 00:48:37.943
and that makes them intolerable to the human body.

00:48:37.943 --> 00:48:38.776
Have you heard that one?

00:48:38.776 --> 00:48:41.943
I've heard that one online again, not with my patients.

00:48:41.943 --> 00:48:42.853
I think it's, (laughs)

00:48:42.853 --> 00:48:45.453
I think a lot of extreme ideas tend to come

00:48:45.453 --> 00:48:46.773
from online spaces

00:48:46.773 --> 00:48:50.043
and perhaps I don't know why that would be,

00:48:50.043 --> 00:48:53.443
but I think if we look at the properties of plants,

00:48:53.443 --> 00:48:56.893
yes they do have certain sort of phytonutrients

00:48:56.893 --> 00:48:59.953
that we benefit from, but the toxins idea,

00:48:59.953 --> 00:49:02.903
I think is actually something that is really damaging

00:49:02.903 --> 00:49:06.433
because often it's those plant so-called toxins that

00:49:06.433 --> 00:49:10.413
provide us with really important factors for our own health,

00:49:10.413 --> 00:49:14.003
cause our bodies can use them in ways that actually help us

00:49:14.003 --> 00:49:17.743
to support our microbiome and our immune systems.

00:49:17.743 --> 00:49:20.493
So I think that's the difference between perhaps

00:49:20.493 --> 00:49:23.223
mechanistic data and actually seeing what people do

00:49:23.223 --> 00:49:25.313
in real life and seeing what they eat in real life

00:49:25.313 --> 00:49:26.683
for longevity and health,

00:49:26.683 --> 00:49:30.243
which are two sometimes completely opposed ideas.

00:49:30.243 --> 00:49:31.563
When everything comes together,

00:49:31.563 --> 00:49:35.673
when mechanistic data and observational data and randomized

00:49:35.673 --> 00:49:38.683
control trials, all come together pointing to one direction,

00:49:38.683 --> 00:49:40.283
we can feel a lot more confident.

00:49:40.283 --> 00:49:42.843
Whereas if we're just looking at a single trial

00:49:42.843 --> 00:49:45.693
or a mechanistic trial on an animal, for example,

00:49:45.693 --> 00:49:47.943
we're not gonna really have the same picture.

