01:00:54.543 --> 01:00:56.993
and muscle pain can be attributable

01:00:56.993 --> 01:01:00.083
to those hormonal changes, up to 30%

01:01:00.083 --> 01:01:03.353
of women going through menopause get body aches,

01:01:03.353 --> 01:01:04.733
but they won't always attributed

01:01:04.733 --> 01:01:07.073
to those changes in hormones,

01:01:07.073 --> 01:01:10.685
which is another big one, dry skin, dry eyes.

01:01:10.685 --> 01:01:14.193
Some women only experience severe dry eyes,

01:01:14.193 --> 01:01:17.303
which can again be very debilitating.

01:01:17.303 --> 01:01:20.603
We're having to use drops, not being able to see properly.

01:01:20.603 --> 01:01:23.436
It can actually really impact quality of life as well.

01:01:24.353 --> 01:01:28.123
Low libido, your sex drive can sometimes go down.

01:01:28.123 --> 01:01:30.053
Understandably, if you have vaginal atrophy,

01:01:30.053 --> 01:01:32.403
but sometimes there are other things attached to that

01:01:32.403 --> 01:01:35.503
as well, with regard to identity

01:01:35.503 --> 01:01:39.503
and feeling of worth and attractiveness to your partner,

01:01:39.503 --> 01:01:40.903
that there's all sorts of other things

01:01:40.903 --> 01:01:43.236
that can sort of play a role there.

01:01:44.093 --> 01:01:46.888
So yeah, there's quite a lot of different symptoms.

01:01:46.888 --> 01:01:49.723
You are more likely to get constipated

01:01:49.723 --> 01:01:53.993
because there are estrogen receptors that are in the gut.

01:01:53.993 --> 01:01:55.963
So you have reduced gut motility,

01:01:55.963 --> 01:01:58.025
which then has an impact on, again,

01:01:58.025 --> 01:02:00.600
your hormone regulation.

01:02:00.600 --> 01:02:03.105
Because of the lack of estrogen,

01:02:03.105 --> 01:02:05.393
your body tries to come back to balance.

01:02:05.393 --> 01:02:07.833
And so you get metabolic changes as well

01:02:07.833 --> 01:02:11.743
in the Western world, about 65% of women have obesity

01:02:11.743 --> 01:02:14.243
because of the way that the fat deposits more

01:02:14.243 --> 01:02:16.846
around sort of the center of the body as a

01:02:16.846 --> 01:02:19.813
way of trying to compensate for the lower estrogen

01:02:19.813 --> 01:02:21.813
that's being produced by the ovaries.

01:02:21.813 --> 01:02:23.133
because as we talked about earlier,

01:02:23.133 --> 01:02:25.353
fat cells can produce estrogen.

01:02:25.353 --> 01:02:26.623
So again, these are things

01:02:26.623 --> 01:02:28.823
that a lot of women don't necessarily realize

01:02:28.823 --> 01:02:31.066
are related to those changes in hormones.

01:02:32.231 --> 01:02:35.953
Bone loss, so unfortunately from about 40 onwards

01:02:35.953 --> 01:02:38.933
we'll lose about half percent of our bone mineral density.

01:02:38.933 --> 01:02:43.817
But when you go through menopause that drops precipitously,

01:02:43.817 --> 01:02:48.303
many women will lose about 20% of their bone mineral density

01:02:48.303 --> 01:02:50.705
in the first five to seven years after menopause.

01:02:50.705 --> 01:02:51.538
Wow.

01:02:51.538 --> 01:02:53.433
And that causes you increased risk, significant fractures.

01:02:53.433 --> 01:02:55.233
Yeah, that's very significant.

01:02:55.233 --> 01:02:56.873
Yeah, it's really significant.

01:02:56.873 --> 01:03:00.083
So I think that this is a public health issue,

01:03:00.083 --> 01:03:03.833
and I think that if more women knew

01:03:03.833 --> 01:03:08.747
that the time around menopause can be a time of empowerment,

01:03:08.747 --> 01:03:10.443
then they could actually potentially

01:03:10.443 --> 01:03:11.816
prevent further problems.

01:03:12.858 --> 01:03:15.893
So HRT or HRT is something that, again,

01:03:15.893 --> 01:03:19.009
we don't tend to talk much about, but is a.

01:03:19.009 --> 01:03:20.643
Hormone replacement therapy.

01:03:20.643 --> 01:03:21.973
Hormone replacement therapy.

01:03:21.973 --> 01:03:24.013
It's a really important thing to open up a conversation

01:03:24.013 --> 01:03:27.163
around because it's one of the number one

01:03:27.163 --> 01:03:30.893
treatments to ameliorate a lot of these symptoms in women

01:03:30.893 --> 01:03:33.418
who are eligible and who want to.

01:03:33.418 --> 01:03:36.703
And including things like bone, mineral, density, loss,

01:03:36.703 --> 01:03:39.093
and heart disease risk as well.

01:03:39.093 --> 01:03:42.033
And I think there's a lost generation of women

01:03:42.033 --> 01:03:46.273
who felt that it was too dangerous to even consider HRT.

01:03:46.273 --> 01:03:48.353
And in those women, I think

01:03:48.353 --> 01:03:50.963
there's a huge injustice that has been done there

01:03:50.963 --> 01:03:55.893
because HRT as a hormone replacement model

01:03:55.893 --> 01:04:00.543
is a great way to ease that hormonal transition.

01:04:00.543 --> 01:04:02.233
Rather than going through all those fluctuations,

01:04:02.233 --> 01:04:04.083
you are replacing a lot of the hormones

01:04:04.083 --> 01:04:05.603
that you would've otherwise lost,

01:04:05.603 --> 01:04:09.166
and you are maintaining your bone mineral density.

01:04:09.166 --> 01:04:12.540
You can improve a lot of the bone ache issues.

01:04:12.540 --> 01:04:14.513
You're actually reducing potentially your

01:04:14.513 --> 01:04:16.223
risk of heart disease as well.

01:04:16.223 --> 01:04:18.313
But there is a window of opportunity with regard

01:04:18.313 --> 01:04:19.713
to heart disease risk.

01:04:19.713 --> 01:04:22.012
And that's like around the menopause,

01:04:22.012 --> 01:04:24.083
if you're within 10 years of menopause

01:04:24.083 --> 01:04:25.653
or less than the age of 60,

01:04:25.653 --> 01:04:27.863
that would be a good time to

01:04:27.863 --> 01:04:31.173
reduce your risk of heart disease moving on in life.

01:04:31.173 --> 01:04:33.223
And the same with the bone mineral density loss.

01:04:33.223 --> 01:04:35.913
There's that window of opportunity there in that

01:04:35.913 --> 01:04:40.611
sort of five to 10 year window between sort of 45, 55,

01:04:40.611 --> 01:04:42.881
where if you are eligible,

01:04:42.881 --> 01:04:47.209
it's worth at least exploring the idea of HRT.

01:04:47.209 --> 01:04:50.586
Okay so with that in mind then, what,

01:04:51.681 --> 01:04:53.073
wait, hold on a second,

01:04:53.073 --> 01:04:55.753
let me collect my thoughts before I start.

01:04:55.753 --> 01:04:57.360
We were talking about HRT, right?

01:04:57.360 --> 01:04:58.193
Right.

01:05:01.076 --> 01:05:02.973
So with that in mind,

01:05:02.973 --> 01:05:07.123
how safe or unsafe is HRT in general now,

01:05:07.123 --> 01:05:10.143
and what are the factors that determine

01:05:10.143 --> 01:05:13.153
whether a woman is a good candidate for this?

01:05:13.153 --> 01:05:15.673
So there will be certain women that are

01:05:15.673 --> 01:05:17.853
not eligible for HRT.

01:05:17.853 --> 01:05:21.193
Primarily those who've experienced breast cancer,

01:05:21.193 --> 01:05:23.053
but there are other potential reasons

01:05:23.053 --> 01:05:25.553
why they wouldn't want to have HRT as well,

01:05:25.553 --> 01:05:29.193
obvious things like pregnancy or a recent blood clot

01:05:29.193 --> 01:05:31.693
or an undiagnosed bleed, for example.

01:05:31.693 --> 01:05:36.483
But in actual fact, I would say excluding those women,

01:05:36.483 --> 01:05:41.483
most women would be pretty good candidates for HRT.

01:05:41.563 --> 01:05:45.463
The main problem that really scared a lot of women

01:05:45.463 --> 01:05:47.247
back in sort of 2002

01:05:47.247 --> 01:05:50.913
was the women's health initiative study,

01:05:50.913 --> 01:05:53.513
which showed that there was potential increased

01:05:53.513 --> 01:05:54.543
risk of breast cancer.

01:05:54.543 --> 01:05:57.483
In fact, the study was halted early

01:05:57.483 --> 01:06:00.863
over concerns about excess breast cancer risk.

01:06:00.863 --> 01:06:05.863
And unfortunately the headlines around that study

01:06:06.053 --> 01:06:07.373
went around the globe

01:06:07.373 --> 01:06:12.373
and made the use of HRT drop by about 66%.

01:06:13.273 --> 01:06:15.113
In other words, the headlines being something

01:06:15.113 --> 01:06:18.453
along the lines of HRT will cause breast cancer.

01:06:18.453 --> 01:06:19.533
Yeah, exactly.

01:06:19.533 --> 01:06:21.653
And it scared everyone.

01:06:21.653 --> 01:06:25.453
It scared doctors. It scared women who were taking HRT.

01:06:25.453 --> 01:06:27.253
So many women stopped taking it.

01:06:27.253 --> 01:06:29.193
But interestingly, Professor Langa

01:06:29.193 --> 01:06:32.503
who was one of the main investigators as part of the study,

01:06:32.503 --> 01:06:35.733
wrote a paper condemning the headline of the study

01:06:35.733 --> 01:06:38.803
as being irresponsible and wholly inaccurate.

01:06:38.803 --> 01:06:41.503
And many of the lead investigators on that study

01:06:41.503 --> 01:06:44.493
were not consulted before it was even published.

01:06:44.493 --> 01:06:47.363
And one of the main problems with the study

01:06:47.363 --> 01:06:51.313
was that they used women aged 65 or thereabouts,

01:06:51.313 --> 01:06:53.813
and they were starting HRT brand new.

01:06:53.813 --> 01:06:55.243
And of course by that stage,

01:06:55.243 --> 01:06:57.593
a lot of those risk factors for breast cancer

01:06:57.593 --> 01:06:59.703
and excess risk of heart disease, for example,

01:06:59.703 --> 01:07:03.393
were already in existence before they began the treatment.

01:07:03.393 --> 01:07:08.393
And I do believe it's let down a whole generation of women.

01:07:08.493 --> 01:07:11.543
There was a really interesting study in the BMJ from 2012

01:07:11.543 --> 01:07:16.543
that showed that if you take HRT for a period of five years,

01:07:16.813 --> 01:07:18.103
you actually have reduced risk

01:07:18.103 --> 01:07:19.593
of heart attacks and heart failure.

01:07:19.593 --> 01:07:21.643
There was no increased risk of breast cancer

01:07:21.643 --> 01:07:23.353
or blood clots at all.

01:07:23.353 --> 01:07:25.933
And there was an interesting study by David Katz

01:07:25.933 --> 01:07:28.126
actually in 2013.

01:07:29.023 --> 01:07:33.543
And he estimated that in the 20 years after 2002,

01:07:34.470 --> 01:07:36.363
that there were anywhere between

01:07:36.363 --> 01:07:38.653
around 20,000 to 90,000 women

01:07:38.653 --> 01:07:42.793
who died prematurely from not having access to HRT.

01:07:42.793 --> 01:07:44.047
Wow.

01:07:44.047 --> 01:07:45.103
Because of the protection it can offer

01:07:45.103 --> 01:07:48.133
for heart disease and bone mineral density loss,

01:07:48.133 --> 01:07:50.293
as well as obviously all of the other symptoms

01:07:50.293 --> 01:07:51.533
that we've talked about

01:07:51.533 --> 01:07:53.983
with the flushing and the mood changes

01:07:53.983 --> 01:07:56.513
and the way the vaginal atrophy

01:07:56.513 --> 01:07:58.043
and the way that these things can impact

01:07:58.043 --> 01:07:59.683
their lives day to day.

01:07:59.683 --> 01:08:02.283
And the optimal time to begin HRT

01:08:02.283 --> 01:08:04.053
would be early in menopause right

01:08:04.053 --> 01:08:06.323
before you've already suffered bone loss

01:08:06.323 --> 01:08:08.773
and all these other ramifications.

01:08:08.773 --> 01:08:12.963
Yeah, and the age of menopause does vary.

01:08:12.963 --> 01:08:15.523
The average age is about 51,

01:08:15.523 --> 01:08:17.003
but it's normal for it to start anywhere

01:08:17.003 --> 01:08:19.160
between ages sort of 45 to 55.

01:08:19.160 --> 01:08:23.803
And I think that it's actually vital to think about

01:08:23.803 --> 01:08:26.653
if you're going to think about starting it then,

01:08:26.653 --> 01:08:31.073
because you are preventing, it's a prevention, isn't it?

01:08:31.073 --> 01:08:33.183
So you're preventing the bone mineral density loss.

01:08:33.183 --> 01:08:35.033
You're preventing the atherosclerotic plaques

01:08:35.033 --> 01:08:35.963
that we start forming

01:08:35.963 --> 01:08:37.653
because of the lack of estrogen in the body and

01:08:37.653 --> 01:08:39.554
how that's cardioprotective.

01:08:39.554 --> 01:08:44.493
And it's a way of then essentially lengthening your time

01:08:44.493 --> 01:08:45.713
where you are sort of functional

01:08:45.713 --> 01:08:47.963
and you're not having some of these problems.

01:08:48.973 --> 01:08:52.633
And what is the composition like,

01:08:52.633 --> 01:08:55.483
what is the hormonal composition of HRT?

01:08:55.483 --> 01:08:57.493
So it is basically a combination

01:08:57.493 --> 01:08:59.593
of estrogen and progesterone.

01:08:59.593 --> 01:09:03.013
Interestingly, if you have estrogen-only HRT,

01:09:03.013 --> 01:09:05.053
then there's absolutely no excess risk

01:09:05.053 --> 01:09:06.913
of breast cancer at all.

01:09:06.913 --> 01:09:09.873
But the reason that progesterone is often added is to help

01:09:09.873 --> 01:09:13.623
protect the womb lining because if you have a normal womb

01:09:13.623 --> 01:09:16.243
and you just give unopposed oral estrogen,

01:09:16.243 --> 01:09:19.173
then you could have excess risk of bleeding

01:09:19.173 --> 01:09:21.593
and increased risk of endometrial cancer,

01:09:21.593 --> 01:09:24.323
which is why progesterone is given in combination

01:09:24.323 --> 01:09:26.106
with the estrogen replacement.

01:09:27.783 --> 01:09:29.653
But if you look at the,

01:09:29.653 --> 01:09:33.126
there's loads of different types and formulations of HRT,

01:09:33.126 --> 01:09:35.223
but I do wanna stress that if you're going

01:09:35.223 --> 01:09:36.583
for like the gold standard,

01:09:36.583 --> 01:09:40.553
which would be an estrogen gel or an estrogen patch

01:09:40.553 --> 01:09:43.763
alongside micronized progesterone,

01:09:43.763 --> 01:09:46.443
then taking that for about five years,

01:09:46.443 --> 01:09:48.923
there was a systematic review to say that there was

01:09:48.923 --> 01:09:52.453
absolutely no increased risk of breast cancer at all.

01:09:52.453 --> 01:09:55.793
So I'd love for women to know that.

01:09:55.793 --> 01:09:57.893
If they want to look into it, they should.

01:10:00.706 --> 01:10:03.476
It comes, as I say, in the form of patches, gels, tablets.

01:10:04.752 --> 01:10:07.913
And yeah, there's loads of different brands,

01:10:07.913 --> 01:10:11.289
but essentially it's just estrogen and progestogen.

01:10:11.289 --> 01:10:15.473
And what percentage of women currently are on HRT?

01:10:15.473 --> 01:10:16.403
Is it a low percentage?

01:10:16.403 --> 01:10:18.863
It's low and it's increasing gradually.

01:10:18.863 --> 01:10:20.423
I think it was probably at its lowest

01:10:20.423 --> 01:10:22.986
just after the 2002 study.

01:10:24.063 --> 01:10:26.513
It's gradually creeping up with a lot more awareness now

01:10:26.513 --> 01:10:30.053
about menopause and the symptoms, especially in the UK.

01:10:30.053 --> 01:10:33.498
I dunno if that's translated over here in such

01:10:33.498 --> 01:10:38.498
a big way, but I get lots more women coming to me

01:10:38.523 --> 01:10:41.863
in clinic asking about HRT, which I'm really pleased about

01:10:41.863 --> 01:10:43.700
because so many women over the years are said,

01:10:43.700 --> 01:10:45.696
"Oh, it's not natural, it's not for me.

01:10:46.727 --> 01:10:50.223
And it's harmful, I'm worried about breast cancer."

01:10:50.223 --> 01:10:54.023
And I want women to know that it's a very valid option

01:10:54.023 --> 01:10:58.363
and it's actually, the menopause guidelines suggests

01:10:58.363 --> 01:10:59.703
that it's the number one treatment

01:10:59.703 --> 01:11:02.597
for bone mineral density loss, as I mentioned.

01:11:02.597 --> 01:11:04.703
Wow, yeah, you mentioned the word natural.

01:11:04.703 --> 01:11:06.843
There is an interesting thing within wellness

01:11:06.843 --> 01:11:09.070
and holistic health circles

01:11:09.070 --> 01:11:12.973
that obviously are gonna prioritize natural remedies.

01:11:12.973 --> 01:11:15.558
Food as medicine, et cetera,

01:11:15.558 --> 01:11:20.183
at the cost of medical interventions that could be helpful.

01:11:20.183 --> 01:11:23.673
So there's a weird in the Venn Diagram

01:11:23.673 --> 01:11:25.713
between evidence-based medicine

01:11:26.639 --> 01:11:29.923
and lifestyle protocols and the kind of organic wellness,

01:11:29.923 --> 01:11:32.496
alternative holistic health,

01:11:33.666 --> 01:11:36.883
I could see attention between those two things like somebody

01:11:36.883 --> 01:11:39.153
who's like, well, I'm just gonna eat organic food.

01:11:39.153 --> 01:11:39.986
You're saying yes,

01:11:39.986 --> 01:11:41.503
but this intervention could be beneficial,

01:11:41.503 --> 01:11:44.083
but Hey, that's not really part of my protocol.

01:11:44.083 --> 01:11:49.083
I'm trying to not be on any kind of exogenous interventions.

01:11:50.183 --> 01:11:52.933
Yeah and that's something that I respect.

01:11:52.933 --> 01:11:55.466
If someone doesn't want to take extra hormones,

01:11:55.466 --> 01:11:57.848
then they shouldn't have to.

01:11:57.848 --> 01:12:00.743
And I completely understand that perspective.

01:12:00.743 --> 01:12:05.283
I think what bothers me is the idea that the choice of women

01:12:05.283 --> 01:12:08.840
is often taken away because of unfounded fears.

01:12:08.840 --> 01:12:10.229
And I think for me,

01:12:10.229 --> 01:12:13.573
it's important to be able to offer something that could

01:12:13.573 --> 01:12:15.493
benefit and whether women want to have it

01:12:15.493 --> 01:12:16.933
or not is up to them,

01:12:16.933 --> 01:12:19.053
but we have to consider what's really natural,

01:12:19.053 --> 01:12:21.601
I guess, in the environment that we're currently living in.

01:12:21.601 --> 01:12:25.323
You could say it's natural to die in childbirth

01:12:25.323 --> 01:12:27.573
because if you are in Afghanistan

01:12:27.573 --> 01:12:29.263
and you're in a village somewhere,

01:12:29.263 --> 01:12:32.523
you've got about a one in 14 chance of dying

01:12:32.523 --> 01:12:34.673
from childbirth, is that natural or yes,

01:12:34.673 --> 01:12:36.913
I suppose it is, but it's clearly not something

01:12:36.913 --> 01:12:39.133
that anybody would wish to happen.

01:12:39.133 --> 01:12:44.133
And so I think when it comes to severe menopausal symptoms,

01:12:44.903 --> 01:12:47.594
but also longevity,

01:12:47.594 --> 01:12:50.493
I wanna be able to provide women with every possible option

01:12:50.493 --> 01:12:52.643
that is deemed to be healthy and appropriate.

01:12:52.643 --> 01:12:54.646
And if they wanna take it, they can.

01:12:55.593 --> 01:12:59.303
You mentioned earlier that the age at which

01:13:00.272 --> 01:13:02.743
young women are experiencing their period

01:13:02.743 --> 01:13:04.583
is getting earlier and earlier.

01:13:04.583 --> 01:13:08.383
Is there also a change in the window for menopause?

01:13:08.383 --> 01:13:11.053
Is that happening earlier also?

01:13:11.053 --> 01:13:14.103
No so I think it may be again,

01:13:14.103 --> 01:13:16.823
a reflection of Western society and the various exposures

01:13:16.823 --> 01:13:20.263
that we have, but menopause in some cultures

01:13:20.263 --> 01:13:23.331
is actually seemingly getting a bit later.

01:13:23.331 --> 01:13:27.912
There is some potential genetic differences as well,

01:13:27.912 --> 01:13:31.713
Afro-American women will potentially,

01:13:31.713 --> 01:13:32.913
African, or should I say,

01:13:32.913 --> 01:13:35.703
I dunno what you say here, African American.

01:13:35.703 --> 01:13:37.223
African American, black women.

01:13:37.223 --> 01:13:39.323
There are some potential genetic differences

01:13:39.323 --> 01:13:41.733
in when you would start the menopause anyway.

01:13:41.733 --> 01:13:43.453
So for example,

01:13:43.453 --> 01:13:45.763
black women may find that they start their periods

01:13:45.763 --> 01:13:48.022
on average about 48, 49,

01:13:48.022 --> 01:13:52.954
whereas white women may start on average about 50, 51.

01:13:52.954 --> 01:13:57.954
But yeah, I think menopause can vary based on diet as well.

01:13:58.163 --> 01:13:59.653
There was an interesting study to show

01:13:59.653 --> 01:14:01.743
that eating more vegetables

01:14:01.743 --> 01:14:04.543
could potentially bring the age of menopause

01:14:04.543 --> 01:14:07.193
slightly younger, again,

01:14:07.193 --> 01:14:09.953
not dramatically like maybe three months on average

01:14:09.953 --> 01:14:12.353
when were looking at sort of large population data.

01:14:12.353 --> 01:14:14.483
But I think that that's very interesting, cause again,

01:14:14.483 --> 01:14:18.143
it speaks to the effect that our diets can have

01:14:18.143 --> 01:14:20.725
on our normal physiological processes.

01:14:20.725 --> 01:14:24.573
Is there a mode of eating or particular foods

01:14:24.573 --> 01:14:27.363
that could lessen some of the

01:14:27.363 --> 01:14:29.545
negative symptomology around it?

01:14:29.545 --> 01:14:32.013
Absolutely and I'd say again,

01:14:32.013 --> 01:14:34.253
I'm gonna bang on about fiber.

01:14:34.253 --> 01:14:38.973
Fiber is crucial and soy, minimally processed soy products

01:14:38.973 --> 01:14:40.853
are really beneficial as well,

01:14:40.853 --> 01:14:42.323
making sure that you have a nice variety

01:14:42.323 --> 01:14:44.883
of things like nuts and seeds for extra minerals,

01:14:44.883 --> 01:14:46.806
like selenium are important.

01:14:47.690 --> 01:14:50.283
Even sort of things like I know we haven't

01:14:50.283 --> 01:14:51.973
touched on thyroid function, but again,

01:14:51.973 --> 01:14:55.103
that's another hormonally regulated organ

01:14:55.103 --> 01:14:56.933
you wanna be able to make sure that you're having

01:14:56.933 --> 01:14:59.583
great sources of iodine, selenium, zinc.

01:14:59.583 --> 01:15:02.223
And that's true for all of the hormones in the body,

01:15:02.223 --> 01:15:04.313
but especially things like the thyroid.

01:15:04.313 --> 01:15:06.683
And what are some of your favorite foods

01:15:06.683 --> 01:15:08.723
that are high in those things?

01:15:08.723 --> 01:15:09.863
So with selenium,

01:15:09.863 --> 01:15:12.299
you could just have like a Brazil nut a day, Brazil nuts.

01:15:12.299 --> 01:15:15.013
That would be pretty good, but it's also in whole grains,

01:15:15.013 --> 01:15:17.523
whole grain rice, whole grain bread,

01:15:17.523 --> 01:15:21.436
other nuts and seeds and the same with zinc as well,

01:15:22.383 --> 01:15:24.763
which again is important for things like sperm production.

01:15:24.763 --> 01:15:26.833
I know we're not talking about men, but sperm.

01:15:26.833 --> 01:15:29.343
We're gonna get to that.

01:15:29.343 --> 01:15:32.043
Yeah, so there are loads of great foods.

01:15:32.043 --> 01:15:34.543
So just think fruits, veggies, whole grains,

01:15:34.543 --> 01:15:38.383
beans, soy products, I'm a big fan of flaxseeds

01:15:38.383 --> 01:15:40.983
just because they're great at helping to improve

01:15:40.983 --> 01:15:42.493
the suppleness of blood vessels.

01:15:42.493 --> 01:15:46.563
And yeah, they've got loads of great omega 3s

01:15:46.563 --> 01:15:48.153
from plant sources as well.

01:15:48.153 --> 01:15:50.203
Just make sure you grind them first grind.

01:15:50.203 --> 01:15:52.073
Otherwise they pass right through.

01:15:52.073 --> 01:15:52.926
Exactly.

01:15:54.173 --> 01:15:56.383
Let's talk about male hormonal health

01:15:56.383 --> 01:15:59.893
is man-opause like a real thing?

01:15:59.893 --> 01:16:02.423
And also I'm interested in your thoughts

01:16:02.423 --> 01:16:06.283
on TRT cause it's obviously the other side of that coin.

01:16:06.283 --> 01:16:09.243
Yeah, so I think that for men, I mean,

01:16:09.243 --> 01:16:11.376
I don't have personal experience of this,

01:16:12.353 --> 01:16:13.693
but certainly with my patients,

01:16:13.693 --> 01:16:15.293
it doesn't seem to be as much of a thing

01:16:15.293 --> 01:16:16.643
for men as for women.

01:16:16.643 --> 01:16:17.553
And I mentioned earlier,

01:16:17.553 --> 01:16:20.513
I think it's because it's a very gradual decline.

01:16:20.513 --> 01:16:22.836
Like with most of the hormones in our body,

01:16:23.763 --> 01:16:27.006
testosterone production can start to wane over time.

01:16:27.957 --> 01:16:30.843
So I think in certain conditions

01:16:30.843 --> 01:16:33.213
it might be beneficial for testosterone replacement,

01:16:33.213 --> 01:16:35.393
but I don't see it as being something

01:16:36.453 --> 01:16:38.643
as vital for male health

01:16:38.643 --> 01:16:41.403
as estrogen replacement would be for female health

01:16:41.403 --> 01:16:44.593
based on the changes in the body that occur for women

01:16:44.593 --> 01:16:46.443
that just simply don't occur for men.

01:16:47.311 --> 01:16:49.643
I know so many guys my age

01:16:49.643 --> 01:16:52.083
that are on testosterone therapy,

01:16:52.083 --> 01:16:55.873
I'm not, but they talk about it.

01:16:55.873 --> 01:16:58.049
It's something that guys of my age tend to,

01:16:58.049 --> 01:16:59.733
"Are you doing that?

01:16:59.733 --> 01:17:01.913
Should I do that, should I not do that?"

01:17:01.913 --> 01:17:05.493
Are there negative health outcomes with respect to that?

01:17:05.493 --> 01:17:07.253
My understanding is that if you get on it,

01:17:07.253 --> 01:17:10.013
then that's it, you have to stay on it because obviously

01:17:10.013 --> 01:17:12.383
if you're taking it exogenously,

01:17:12.383 --> 01:17:14.983
then it's gonna shut down your body's

01:17:14.983 --> 01:17:17.353
whatever your body is making, right?

01:17:17.353 --> 01:17:18.503
Like it's telling your body,

01:17:18.503 --> 01:17:19.863
like you don't need to make any of this

01:17:19.863 --> 01:17:21.683
because you're getting it.

01:17:21.683 --> 01:17:24.283
I think this is where men should be cautious

01:17:24.283 --> 01:17:26.073
and speak to their own doctors about whether

01:17:26.073 --> 01:17:27.233
it's necessary or not.

01:17:27.233 --> 01:17:29.523
Especially if you are somebody that is actually hoping

01:17:29.523 --> 01:17:31.723
to perhaps further a child at any time,

01:17:31.723 --> 01:17:32.733
because like you say,

01:17:32.733 --> 01:17:35.193
you are turning off your own testosterone production there,

01:17:35.193 --> 01:17:37.602
whatever it is still going on

01:17:37.602 --> 01:17:41.683
and that can have ramifications for your fertility.

01:17:41.683 --> 01:17:44.673
So I think that probably be the main consideration

01:17:44.673 --> 01:17:47.503
depending on the intentions of the person

01:17:47.503 --> 01:17:49.233
that wants to take testosterone replacement,

01:17:49.233 --> 01:17:50.313
just to think carefully about

01:17:50.313 --> 01:17:52.683
if it could actually impact their fertility.

01:17:52.683 --> 01:17:53.516
But yeah.

01:17:53.516 --> 01:17:55.563
I think most guys, my age are pro are,

01:17:55.563 --> 01:17:57.273
they're not worried about that part.

01:17:57.273 --> 01:18:00.793
Probably actually they want the opposite. (laughs)

01:18:00.793 --> 01:18:01.953
Not everyone, but.

01:18:01.953 --> 01:18:03.808
Yeah so yeah it's not something

01:18:03.808 --> 01:18:06.203
that is as prevalent or indeed

01:18:06.203 --> 01:18:08.724
I don't believe is necessary for men as it is for women,

01:18:08.724 --> 01:18:11.906
but it's a conversation that I think is occurring

01:18:11.906 --> 01:18:13.813
a lot more in-

01:18:13.813 --> 01:18:16.263
Wellness what would be the reason for doing it?

01:18:17.333 --> 01:18:22.333
Probably to increase things like muscle mass, mood changes

01:18:22.723 --> 01:18:26.943
sometimes that can occur in men as the testosterone levels

01:18:26.943 --> 01:18:29.676
that you're endogenously producing fall.

01:18:30.825 --> 01:18:33.853
I think one of the main reasons that my patients

01:18:33.853 --> 01:18:35.273
would go on testosterone replacement

01:18:35.273 --> 01:18:39.093
is if they have excess estrogen exposures.

01:18:39.093 --> 01:18:40.393
So it tends to happen more

01:18:41.413 --> 01:18:44.513
when men are carrying excess weight as well.

01:18:44.513 --> 01:18:46.873
That because as I mentioned before,

01:18:46.873 --> 01:18:49.470
the fat cells can produce estrogen,

01:18:49.470 --> 01:18:52.913
which can have perhaps a slight feminizing effect.

01:18:52.913 --> 01:18:53.993
So yeah.

01:18:53.993 --> 01:18:57.933
What else is unique to male hormone health

01:18:57.933 --> 01:18:59.729
that you can speak to?

01:18:59.729 --> 01:19:02.033
I think one of the main things that is worth talking about

01:19:02.033 --> 01:19:06.298
for male hormone health is the gradual decline

01:19:06.298 --> 01:19:10.293
or the gradual apparent decline in sperm counts

01:19:10.293 --> 01:19:13.063
over the course of the last couple of generations

01:19:13.063 --> 01:19:14.653
and the increased rates

01:19:14.653 --> 01:19:18.123
of something called hypospadias which is where the urethra,

01:19:18.123 --> 01:19:21.023
the wee tube, sort of forms and grows

01:19:21.023 --> 01:19:22.503
on the underside of the penis

01:19:23.993 --> 01:19:27.243
and the increased rates of

01:19:27.243 --> 01:19:30.193
only having one testicle at birth.

01:19:30.193 --> 01:19:34.703
This is something that I suspect is related to, again,

01:19:34.703 --> 01:19:36.663
the plastics exposures in our environment,

01:19:36.663 --> 01:19:39.723
the phthalate exposures, and that-

01:19:39.723 --> 01:19:41.083
I didn't know that was a thing.

01:19:41.083 --> 01:19:44.483
An increase in men being born with one testicle.

01:19:44.483 --> 01:19:47.416
Yep what percentage of the population?

01:19:47.416 --> 01:19:48.249
I don't know the percent.

01:19:48.249 --> 01:19:50.613
Significant enough that it's-

01:19:50.613 --> 01:19:52.088
It's been noted to be increasing.

01:19:52.088 --> 01:19:52.921
Wow.

01:19:52.921 --> 01:19:55.883
And again, as I said, the hypospadias issue as well,

01:19:55.883 --> 01:19:58.483
where there's a sort of a distortion to the penis as a

01:19:58.483 --> 01:20:03.483
result of changes in the exposures in the womb, basically,

01:20:04.453 --> 01:20:06.025
'cause obviously this is the way

01:20:06.025 --> 01:20:07.573
that the baby has been born.

01:20:07.573 --> 01:20:10.713
And yeah, I do see that there is a correlation there

01:20:10.713 --> 01:20:13.393
between that and increased rates of exposure

01:20:13.393 --> 01:20:16.553
to things like microplastics, phthalates, BPA,

01:20:16.553 --> 01:20:18.693
and it can potentially affect sperm counts too.

01:20:18.693 --> 01:20:22.055
So simple things that I think men could do

01:20:22.055 --> 01:20:24.303
that would be useful would be,

01:20:24.303 --> 01:20:26.303
studies are shown that vigorous exercise

01:20:26.303 --> 01:20:28.353
is quite good for sperm count,

01:20:28.353 --> 01:20:30.503
assuming that you're not putting too much excess heat

01:20:30.503 --> 01:20:32.123
on the testicles themselves.

01:20:32.123 --> 01:20:34.583
Because like for example, with cycling or hot baths,

01:20:34.583 --> 01:20:37.203
cause that can have an impact on the sperm production.

01:20:37.203 --> 01:20:39.723
Otherwise vigorous exercise is good.

01:20:39.723 --> 01:20:42.593
Sources of zinc and selenium, as I mentioned,

01:20:42.593 --> 01:20:44.303
things like nuts and seeds are really helpful.

01:20:44.303 --> 01:20:45.853
Co enzyme Q10, again,

01:20:45.853 --> 01:20:48.633
that can come through those kinds of healthy foods,

01:20:48.633 --> 01:20:50.453
nuts, seeds, whole grains.

01:20:50.453 --> 01:20:55.453
And minimizing plastics exposures generally,

01:20:55.483 --> 01:20:57.913
which I think probably the low hanging fruit

01:20:57.913 --> 01:21:01.993
would be to drink your water from glass rather than plastic

01:21:01.993 --> 01:21:05.009
to avoid something that's come in a plastic bottle.

01:21:05.009 --> 01:21:07.343
If it's been sitting in the heat like in your car,

01:21:07.343 --> 01:21:09.283
if it's a hot day, for example.

01:21:09.283 --> 01:21:10.133
And you don't know,

01:21:10.133 --> 01:21:13.823
it could be in a cooler in the grocery store,

01:21:13.823 --> 01:21:15.673
but it had to be transported from somewhere.

01:21:15.673 --> 01:21:18.823
It could have been on a truck for days or weeks.

01:21:18.823 --> 01:21:19.656
That's true.

01:21:19.656 --> 01:21:20.489
Excruciating heat.

01:21:20.489 --> 01:21:21.799
That's true.

01:21:21.799 --> 01:21:22.799
We just don't know.

01:21:22.799 --> 01:21:24.033
Getting rid of plastic.

01:21:24.033 --> 01:21:24.866
It's so hard.

01:21:24.866 --> 01:21:25.799
It's so hard.

01:21:25.799 --> 01:21:27.510
It's so completely ubiquitous.

01:21:27.510 --> 01:21:28.510
It's crazy.

01:21:28.510 --> 01:21:29.343
And I say it's almost impossible.

01:21:29.343 --> 01:21:30.823
I think recently there was a study to show that it's been

01:21:30.823 --> 01:21:33.193
microplastics have been found in the human bloodstream.

01:21:33.193 --> 01:21:35.903
It's something that we can't escape at this point,

01:21:35.903 --> 01:21:37.373
but we can minimize.

01:21:37.373 --> 01:21:40.793
And I don't want people to come away from this conversation

01:21:40.793 --> 01:21:42.593
feeling scared I think I'd rather,

01:21:42.593 --> 01:21:44.943
they feel empowered to know there's a few things

01:21:44.943 --> 01:21:47.733
that we can do to minimize our overall exposure.

01:21:47.733 --> 01:21:49.313
And that would be heating,

01:21:49.313 --> 01:21:51.393
if we are using a microwave, for example,

01:21:51.393 --> 01:21:55.213
then just put it in ceramic or in glass as you heat it.

01:21:55.213 --> 01:21:57.809
If you are drinking water, drink it from a bottle maybe.

01:21:57.809 --> 01:22:00.724
If you can invest in a water filter, do so.

01:22:00.724 --> 01:22:03.303
Simple things that hopefully

01:22:03.303 --> 01:22:07.333
will over years accrue to benefits.

01:22:07.333 --> 01:22:11.883
What about the role of dietary fats and hormone health?

01:22:11.883 --> 01:22:14.513
Yeah interestingly I have read data

01:22:14.513 --> 01:22:16.363
to say that saturated fat consumption

01:22:16.363 --> 01:22:19.013
has been associated with lower sperm counts in men.

01:22:19.013 --> 01:22:22.123
So that's something of interest.

01:22:22.123 --> 01:22:24.183
We also know that when you are eating

01:22:24.183 --> 01:22:25.536
a high saturated fat diet,

01:22:25.536 --> 01:22:28.293
then it tends to be mostly from animal products.

01:22:28.293 --> 01:22:31.093
And again, you've got those extra plastics exposure,

01:22:31.093 --> 01:22:32.933
things that can affect hormonal health from there.

01:22:32.933 --> 01:22:35.183
So it's hard to necessarily separate the two.

01:22:36.283 --> 01:22:38.053
But in a more general sense,

01:22:38.053 --> 01:22:40.749
we have lots of data to show that a saturated fat rich diet

01:22:40.749 --> 01:22:43.983
is very linked to things like heart disease

01:22:43.983 --> 01:22:48.083
and public health campaigns can make a difference.

01:22:48.083 --> 01:22:51.873
So I know that there, may be listeners that think, oh,

01:22:51.873 --> 01:22:53.693
well you're only feeling better

01:22:53.693 --> 01:22:55.373
because you're eating more whole foods

01:22:55.373 --> 01:22:58.803
and it's fine to have things like grass-fed beef and so on.

01:22:58.803 --> 01:23:01.643
But if you look at data from countries like Finland,

01:23:01.643 --> 01:23:04.373
for example, they had the highest rates

01:23:04.373 --> 01:23:05.593
of heart disease in the world

01:23:05.593 --> 01:23:08.423
back in the 1970s, they wanted to do something about it.

01:23:08.423 --> 01:23:11.493
They had a massive public health campaign.

01:23:11.493 --> 01:23:13.853
Many people reduced their red meat consumption,

01:23:13.853 --> 01:23:16.543
stopped smoking, eight more vegetables.

01:23:16.543 --> 01:23:20.093
And then moving fast forward about 20, 30 years,

01:23:20.093 --> 01:23:24.103
they've managed to reduce the heart disease rates by 80%.

01:23:24.103 --> 01:23:27.125
So we can see that these basic simple things that people

01:23:27.125 --> 01:23:29.803
can do that can have tremendous impacts

01:23:29.803 --> 01:23:32.113
on our health moving forwards.

01:23:32.113 --> 01:23:32.983
Right.

01:23:32.983 --> 01:23:35.865
Within the whole food plant-based community,

01:23:35.865 --> 01:23:37.365
there's varying opinions about

01:23:39.483 --> 01:23:44.483
the positive or negative consequences of fats in the diet.

01:23:44.743 --> 01:23:47.643
We have the Caldwell Esselstyn camp,

01:23:47.643 --> 01:23:50.378
which is very low fat, no oils.

01:23:50.378 --> 01:23:52.319
Typically that protocol

01:23:52.319 --> 01:23:55.263
is something that's advised for people

01:23:55.263 --> 01:23:57.023
that are experiencing heart disease

01:23:57.023 --> 01:24:01.379
or have suffered some sort of atherosclerotic event.

01:24:01.379 --> 01:24:04.103
And then there's another camp of people

01:24:04.103 --> 01:24:07.643
who are saying that's hyperbole,

01:24:07.643 --> 01:24:11.163
a healthy dose of olive oil in your diet is fine.

01:24:11.163 --> 01:24:12.980
Like how do you come down on this hot debate?

01:24:12.980 --> 01:24:15.603
It's very divisive.

01:24:15.603 --> 01:24:19.703
It is divisive and I hate divisive debates. (laughs)

01:24:19.703 --> 01:24:21.213
So I try and stay out of them.

01:24:21.213 --> 01:24:22.123
I dunno if you noticed,

01:24:22.123 --> 01:24:24.173
but I don't tend to get into it online.

01:24:24.173 --> 01:24:26.183
Well, I'm gonna put you square in the middle

01:24:26.183 --> 01:24:27.016
of it right now.

01:24:27.016 --> 01:24:28.603
You're gonna have to state your position.

01:24:28.603 --> 01:24:31.753
Okay so my position is we have to eat more veggies,

01:24:31.753 --> 01:24:33.803
as I mentioned before, that's the number one.

01:24:33.803 --> 01:24:37.893
You're dodging, that's a dodge if I ever heard one.

01:24:37.893 --> 01:24:39.273
Looking at the data that we have,

01:24:39.273 --> 01:24:43.913
I think it's fair to say that if you are having good quality

01:24:43.913 --> 01:24:46.373
olive oil with polyphenols in it,

01:24:46.373 --> 01:24:48.473
can have some health benefits.

01:24:48.473 --> 01:24:52.633
I don't think that having olive oil in your diet is

01:24:52.633 --> 01:24:54.473
an unhealthy thing to do.

01:24:54.473 --> 01:24:58.368
And I think a generally Mediterranean style of diet is known

01:24:58.368 --> 01:25:00.133
by many people,

01:25:00.133 --> 01:25:03.143
many nutrition researchers to be one of the best ways

01:25:03.143 --> 01:25:06.236
of eating for overall health, which includes olive oil.

01:25:07.476 --> 01:25:11.833
Yeah, I don't think that for the majority of people

01:25:11.833 --> 01:25:15.153
having oil here and there is going to be at all harmful,

01:25:15.153 --> 01:25:17.223
I'm not somebody that thinks that seed oils

01:25:17.223 --> 01:25:18.993
are particularly harmful either

01:25:18.993 --> 01:25:21.803
because they are rich in PUFAs and MUFAs,

01:25:21.803 --> 01:25:24.663
polyunsaturated fatty acids, mono unsaturated fatty acids,

01:25:24.663 --> 01:25:27.186
which we know are beneficial to health

01:25:27.186 --> 01:25:31.853
if you are replacing saturated fats in the diet

01:25:31.853 --> 01:25:33.893
with those kinds of oils.

01:25:33.893 --> 01:25:36.090
So yeah, I think

01:25:36.090 --> 01:25:38.873
it's hard to sort of come down hard and fast,

01:25:38.873 --> 01:25:41.963
but I'd say looking at the data that we have

01:25:41.963 --> 01:25:44.893
trying to compare, one thing to another,

01:25:44.893 --> 01:25:48.773
seed oils have no demonstrable issues

01:25:48.773 --> 01:25:50.793
when it comes to heart health.

01:25:50.793 --> 01:25:55.521
But I would say that if you are aiming to lose weight,

01:25:55.521 --> 01:25:58.923
then it's not a great idea to have a lot of things

01:25:58.923 --> 01:26:01.253
like seed oils in your diet because they're very calorific

01:26:01.253 --> 01:26:03.193
and they don't really contain a lot

01:26:03.193 --> 01:26:04.333
in terms of the nutrients

01:26:04.333 --> 01:26:06.373
that you'd be looking at to help fill you up

01:26:06.373 --> 01:26:08.403
like the fiber-rich foods would.

01:26:08.403 --> 01:26:10.983
And also I have a certain cohort of patients,

01:26:10.983 --> 01:26:12.413
certainly that notice

01:26:12.413 --> 01:26:13.943
when they have a lot of oils in the diet

01:26:13.943 --> 01:26:17.943
that it can flare up things like inflammatory arthritis.

01:26:17.943 --> 01:26:21.085
And I think it would be remiss of me to avoid mentioning

01:26:21.085 --> 01:26:24.953
that certain people who have a whole food plant-based,

01:26:24.953 --> 01:26:29.193
no-oil diet find that it's beneficial for inflammatory

01:26:29.193 --> 01:26:31.473
arthritis as well as potentially beneficial

01:26:31.473 --> 01:26:33.233
for heart disease.

01:26:33.233 --> 01:26:35.283
And I would assume there's a difference

01:26:36.647 --> 01:26:38.689
between raw olive oil that you sprinkle on a salad

01:26:38.689 --> 01:26:40.363
versus cooking with it,

01:26:40.363 --> 01:26:44.023
like the oxidative impact of high heat

01:26:44.023 --> 01:26:45.533
on some of these oils.

01:26:45.533 --> 01:26:46.843
That's a whole different thing.

01:26:46.843 --> 01:26:47.676
It is.

01:26:47.676 --> 01:26:50.313
Although I would say that the heat at which most people cook

01:26:50.313 --> 01:26:53.143
at home is not gonna cause too much oxidation of olive oil.

01:26:53.143 --> 01:26:56.443
I think that's something that's probably overstated.

01:26:56.443 --> 01:26:58.613
Do what is the oxidation temperature point?

01:26:58.613 --> 01:26:59.446
Do you know?

01:26:59.446 --> 01:27:02.126
I don't remember Rich, you put me on the spot there.

01:27:02.126 --> 01:27:03.385
I stumped you.
Yeah.

01:27:03.385 --> 01:27:04.223
I'd have to look that one up

01:27:04.223 --> 01:27:07.675
I know that, well every oil has a different-

01:27:07.675 --> 01:27:09.993
Smoke point.
Smoke point, right?

01:27:09.993 --> 01:27:12.003
So coconut oil is much higher.

01:27:12.003 --> 01:27:12.836
It is.

01:27:12.836 --> 01:27:14.843
It doesn't oxidize until you get super hot.

01:27:14.843 --> 01:27:17.203
So it's sort of advised that if you're gonna cook with one,

01:27:17.203 --> 01:27:18.363
that's the one to cook with.

01:27:18.363 --> 01:27:19.933
Well, that depends

01:27:19.933 --> 01:27:23.183
because coconut oil also contains saturated fat,

01:27:23.183 --> 01:27:24.793
which is still saturated fat,

01:27:24.793 --> 01:27:26.583
even though it comes from a plant.

01:27:26.583 --> 01:27:30.073
So I think if someone wants to

01:27:30.073 --> 01:27:31.583
concern themselves with the smoke point,

01:27:31.583 --> 01:27:35.153
then avocado oil is probably a good choice

01:27:36.181 --> 01:27:37.293
or cold pressed canola oil would be a good choice as well.

01:27:40.406 --> 01:27:42.824
That's always been the one, the number one enemy one.

01:27:42.824 --> 01:27:45.407
Sorry, but the data suggests

01:27:46.562 --> 01:27:49.062
that cold press canola oil is

01:27:50.103 --> 01:27:52.653
a pretty good oil to cook with because it contains

01:27:52.653 --> 01:27:57.393
the unsaturated fats and it has a higher smoke point.

01:27:57.393 --> 01:27:58.383
So yeah.

01:27:58.383 --> 01:28:01.297
Well, if you don't wanna cook with it, you don't have to.

01:28:01.297 --> 01:28:04.983
Controversy.
(laughs)

01:28:04.983 --> 01:28:07.613
In the daily practice of being a doctor

01:28:07.613 --> 01:28:08.503
and seeing patients,

01:28:08.503 --> 01:28:12.939
what do you find to be when you're in this discussion

01:28:12.939 --> 01:28:14.353
around changing your lifestyle,

01:28:14.353 --> 01:28:18.233
maybe adopting a more plant predominant diet,

01:28:18.233 --> 01:28:19.953
and then following up with these people,

01:28:19.953 --> 01:28:23.643
what do you find to be the common blind spots

01:28:23.643 --> 01:28:28.643
or stumbling blocks or places where people can go astray?

01:28:28.883 --> 01:28:31.473
I think our food environment is probably

01:28:31.473 --> 01:28:32.323
the biggest thing.

01:28:32.323 --> 01:28:37.323
So we seem to have this feeling in the wellness space,

01:28:37.743 --> 01:28:40.003
that we are all individuals and unique

01:28:40.003 --> 01:28:43.163
and we can make choices that are not unrelated

01:28:43.163 --> 01:28:44.333
to other things in the world.

01:28:44.333 --> 01:28:47.453
And actually most of the choices we make around food

01:28:47.453 --> 01:28:49.343
are unconscious choices about,

01:28:49.343 --> 01:28:53.273
I think UCL research stated that there was over 200

01:28:53.273 --> 01:28:56.103
unconscious food choices that we make every day

01:28:56.103 --> 01:28:59.212
and only about 13 to 15 conscious food choices.

01:28:59.212 --> 01:29:00.212
Wow.

01:29:00.212 --> 01:29:03.123
When I didn't know that we made that many food choices

01:29:03.123 --> 01:29:04.323
in a day period.

01:29:04.323 --> 01:29:07.243
Well exactly, but that's what fascinates me is to think

01:29:07.243 --> 01:29:09.773
when the research tells us that

01:29:09.773 --> 01:29:11.956
so many of the factors that control what we eat

01:29:11.956 --> 01:29:15.343
are actually out of our conscious mind,

01:29:15.343 --> 01:29:18.933
it's much harder than to make changes for the long term

01:29:18.933 --> 01:29:19.766
that are healthy,

01:29:19.766 --> 01:29:23.413
especially if you don't have the mental bandwidth

01:29:23.413 --> 01:29:25.513
or the budget to think about these things.

01:29:25.513 --> 01:29:27.890
Having said that, we know that whole plant foods

01:29:27.890 --> 01:29:30.083
can be some of the cheapest things

01:29:30.083 --> 01:29:32.533
you can buy as long as you can get access to them.

01:29:32.533 --> 01:29:34.463
So one of the main stumbling blocks for me

01:29:34.463 --> 01:29:36.853
has always been people's habits

01:29:36.853 --> 01:29:39.923
and ability to have the bandwidth to make

01:29:39.923 --> 01:29:42.023
those changes mentally.

01:29:42.023 --> 01:29:45.753
So I really start simple, just go for very simple swaps,

01:29:45.753 --> 01:29:49.563
find their favorite meals and make them with more plants.

01:29:49.563 --> 01:29:51.623
Basically, maybe get them to write down

01:29:51.623 --> 01:29:52.483
the favorite things that they

01:29:52.483 --> 01:29:54.553
like to cook and add more beans,

01:29:54.553 --> 01:29:57.383
add more lentils, add more vegetables,

01:29:57.383 --> 01:30:00.203
and then gradually crowd out the other things

01:30:00.203 --> 01:30:02.313
has been one of my number one sort of ways

01:30:02.313 --> 01:30:04.496
to get people to eat more veggies.

01:30:05.643 --> 01:30:09.003
I feel like it's two main things.

01:30:09.003 --> 01:30:11.446
One which you just refer to,

01:30:12.493 --> 01:30:16.366
which has a lot to do with being more conscious about

01:30:16.366 --> 01:30:18.833
the environment that you keep.

01:30:18.833 --> 01:30:21.113
If you can create an environment

01:30:21.113 --> 01:30:23.373
where the healthy choice is the most convenient,

01:30:23.373 --> 01:30:25.903
because you've cleared all the bullshit out of your cabinets

01:30:25.903 --> 01:30:28.033
and you only have healthy choices.

01:30:28.033 --> 01:30:30.613
And when you wake up in the middle of the night,

01:30:30.613 --> 01:30:32.053
sorry it's gonna happen,

01:30:32.053 --> 01:30:34.629
you've got the much, you get your carrot, right?

01:30:34.629 --> 01:30:36.243
You know that Ben and Jerry's just ain't there.

01:30:36.243 --> 01:30:39.063
So it's about a conducive environment

01:30:39.063 --> 01:30:40.493
to making the better choice.

01:30:40.493 --> 01:30:43.683
But I think perhaps even the bigger issue

01:30:44.569 --> 01:30:46.163
and stumbling block is really the social issue.

01:30:46.163 --> 01:30:47.733
People get all up in their heads

01:30:47.733 --> 01:30:50.663
about what other people are gonna think about them.

01:30:50.663 --> 01:30:53.523
What's gonna happen when they go to Susie and Dave's

01:30:53.523 --> 01:30:55.663
dinner party and they don't wanna make a fuss,

01:30:55.663 --> 01:30:58.883
or I have to go on vacation with my neighbors

01:30:58.883 --> 01:31:01.653
and it's just gonna be too problematic.

01:31:01.653 --> 01:31:05.213
And people like relinquish their boundaries

01:31:06.399 --> 01:31:09.943
or are just too afraid to stand up for themselves

01:31:09.943 --> 01:31:12.033
or to like make that conscious choice

01:31:12.033 --> 01:31:14.593
because they don't want to be difficult.

01:31:14.593 --> 01:31:16.173
Yeah, I can totally relate

01:31:16.173 --> 01:31:18.243
to that because that was my instinct first of all,

01:31:18.243 --> 01:31:20.113
when I started on this journey.

01:31:20.113 --> 01:31:21.213
My main thought was, oh,

01:31:21.213 --> 01:31:23.133
this is gonna be so awkward for everybody.

01:31:23.133 --> 01:31:25.693
People are gonna think this is so strange.

01:31:25.693 --> 01:31:27.283
I think as social creatures,

01:31:27.283 --> 01:31:28.723
it's one of the main considerations

01:31:28.723 --> 01:31:30.843
that a lot of people have.

01:31:30.843 --> 01:31:33.693
And I guess it comes down to

01:31:34.663 --> 01:31:39.233
living in alignment with the things that you most want.

01:31:39.233 --> 01:31:43.276
And do you most want to be somebody that pleases others?

01:31:44.123 --> 01:31:46.733
Do you most want to be somebody that lives in alignment

01:31:46.733 --> 01:31:48.423
with things that are important to you?

01:31:48.423 --> 01:31:50.563
Are those things one and the same?

01:31:50.563 --> 01:31:53.663
If you can have a conversation with yourself

01:31:54.649 --> 01:31:57.453
around the values that you are living by,

01:31:57.453 --> 01:32:00.933
it becomes much easier to then make conscious choices

01:32:00.933 --> 01:32:03.023
that are in alignment to those values.

01:32:03.023 --> 01:32:05.873
So I would actually encourage everybody

01:32:05.873 --> 01:32:07.933
to have this conversation with their loved ones

01:32:07.933 --> 01:32:09.073
or even just with themselves.

01:32:09.073 --> 01:32:10.603
They could write it down in a journal,

01:32:10.603 --> 01:32:13.013
write down their top values in life

01:32:13.013 --> 01:32:16.543
and how their food choices can relate to those values.

01:32:16.543 --> 01:32:19.379
And then it becomes a lot easier because you're taking away

01:32:19.379 --> 01:32:21.953
a lot of the extra choices that you're having to make,

01:32:21.953 --> 01:32:25.073
or the social situations that you're having to navigate,

01:32:25.073 --> 01:32:27.823
because you are consciously living in alignment

01:32:27.823 --> 01:32:29.293
with the things that matter.

01:32:29.293 --> 01:32:33.173
So if it matters to you to minimize your risk of heart

01:32:33.173 --> 01:32:35.603
disease, because you have a relative that died young,

01:32:35.603 --> 01:32:37.323
you wanna see your children grow up.

01:32:37.323 --> 01:32:40.013
Then you write that down and you find ways

01:32:40.013 --> 01:32:42.453
to make it work within that kind of framework

01:32:42.453 --> 01:32:44.306
if you like your values framework.

01:32:45.173 --> 01:32:47.163
If animal compassion is important to you,

01:32:47.163 --> 01:32:49.343
it's probably a lot easier because you've already made

01:32:49.343 --> 01:32:52.383
that decision for another outside purpose.

01:32:52.383 --> 01:32:54.263
It's something that's outside of yourself.

01:32:54.263 --> 01:32:55.943
And perhaps the same can be true for someone

01:32:55.943 --> 01:32:58.393
that cares deeply about the environment and the planet.

01:32:58.393 --> 01:33:01.143
They've already made that decision based on a value

01:33:01.143 --> 01:33:03.343
that's so strong to them that it actually

01:33:03.343 --> 01:33:05.433
makes it a little bit easier to stick to.

01:33:05.433 --> 01:33:08.973
I think health overall is probably one of the first ways

01:33:08.973 --> 01:33:11.033
people go towards plant-based nutrition,

01:33:11.033 --> 01:33:13.463
but it's also one of the hardest ways to stick with it

01:33:13.463 --> 01:33:17.383
if that's your only goal, because it's quite self focused.

01:33:17.383 --> 01:33:20.013
Whereas if you can focus on something outside of self,

01:33:20.013 --> 01:33:22.723
then it potentially could make those choices a lot easier.

01:33:22.723 --> 01:33:26.263
Either environmental health or animal compassion or reasons

01:33:26.263 --> 01:33:28.393
to stay healthy for others.

01:33:28.393 --> 01:33:30.533
It tends to be in my experience ways

01:33:30.533 --> 01:33:32.303
that make it stick a lot more.

01:33:32.303 --> 01:33:34.603
That's a really good way of thinking about it.

01:33:34.603 --> 01:33:36.483
One of the questions that I get all the time,

01:33:36.483 --> 01:33:39.153
I'm sure you do as well is I want to do this,

01:33:39.153 --> 01:33:41.593
but my partner doesn't want any part of it.

01:33:41.593 --> 01:33:43.313
And you began on this journey.

01:33:43.313 --> 01:33:44.733
Richard was doing it ahead of you

01:33:44.733 --> 01:33:47.433
two different dinners meals, et cetera.

01:33:47.433 --> 01:33:51.303
But for a lot of couples, that becomes very problematic,

01:33:51.303 --> 01:33:53.923
especially when those values might come

01:33:53.923 --> 01:33:55.723
into conflict with each other.

01:33:55.723 --> 01:33:57.193
Yes, you're so right.

01:33:57.193 --> 01:34:01.033
And I think my advice from that being

01:34:01.033 --> 01:34:03.033
the partner that was most reluctant,

01:34:03.033 --> 01:34:04.766
perhaps I can speak to this more.

01:34:05.603 --> 01:34:10.603
If the idea that what I needed was patience and time.

01:34:12.893 --> 01:34:15.713
And sometimes people will never come around.

01:34:15.713 --> 01:34:19.723
Sometimes the people you love the most will never want to do

01:34:19.723 --> 01:34:23.246
the things that are important to you when it comes to diet.

01:34:24.452 --> 01:34:28.503
And I think that taking away that expectation is important

01:34:28.503 --> 01:34:30.873
because when you start to expect things of other people,

01:34:30.873 --> 01:34:32.913
that's where you can start to become very unhappy.

01:34:32.913 --> 01:34:36.923
It builds kind of criticism in your mind.

01:34:36.923 --> 01:34:40.349
It builds comparison. It builds expectation.

01:34:40.349 --> 01:34:43.593
And that can be quite toxic for your relationship.

01:34:43.593 --> 01:34:46.663
So my best advice is just to

01:34:48.119 --> 01:34:48.953
do the thing that's making you happy,

01:34:48.953 --> 01:34:51.003
do the thing that brings you in alignment

01:34:51.898 --> 01:34:53.074
with who you want to be

01:34:53.074 --> 01:34:55.082
and show your partner through your actions,

01:34:55.082 --> 01:34:57.193
how much happier, how much healthier,

01:34:57.193 --> 01:34:59.833
and how much more abundant you feel in your life.

01:34:59.833 --> 01:35:02.683
And then if they wanna come along for the ride, they can.

01:35:03.599 --> 01:35:06.463
Right but you have to be detached from that expectation.

01:35:06.463 --> 01:35:09.113
Otherwise you're gonna be vibing them all the time.

01:35:09.113 --> 01:35:11.517
And then that piousness creeps up

01:35:11.517 --> 01:35:13.113
and then it just becomes a disaster.

01:35:13.113 --> 01:35:15.993
Exactly, you've gotta let that go. (laughs)

01:35:15.993 --> 01:35:17.363
You just gotta let that go.

01:35:17.363 --> 01:35:18.768
Even if it's in the back.

01:35:18.768 --> 01:35:20.823
Well, if I keep doing this, they'll eventually come around.

01:35:20.823 --> 01:35:23.493
It's no, you gotta like get rid of that.

01:35:23.493 --> 01:35:25.803
I do think that that's probably the best way for you

01:35:25.803 --> 01:35:27.793
to sustain a healthy relationship with the people

01:35:27.793 --> 01:35:28.626
that you love the most.

01:35:28.626 --> 01:35:31.023
And it's hard because often the people you love the most

01:35:31.023 --> 01:35:32.883
may you feel, need it the most,

01:35:32.883 --> 01:35:35.323
especially if they have a health condition

01:35:35.323 --> 01:35:37.413
that could benefit from doing that.

01:35:37.413 --> 01:35:39.973
But you have to let it go because it's not your life.

01:35:39.973 --> 01:35:40.949
It's their life.

01:35:40.949 --> 01:35:43.487
Right, that's advice for this particular scenario,

01:35:43.487 --> 01:35:46.433
but for a zillion others as well,

01:35:46.433 --> 01:35:49.683
like in terms of like partnership dynamics.

01:35:49.683 --> 01:35:51.573
And in a partnership,

01:35:51.573 --> 01:35:53.913
you will often have expectations of a partner

01:35:53.913 --> 01:35:55.526
that are unspoken.

01:35:56.460 --> 01:35:59.663
And that could be true for any kind of scenario.

01:35:59.663 --> 01:36:01.693
And that's not to say that you shouldn't communicate the

01:36:01.693 --> 01:36:02.893
things that are important to you.

01:36:02.893 --> 01:36:04.333
Of course you should.

01:36:04.333 --> 01:36:06.743
But it's the expectation that is attached to that,

01:36:06.743 --> 01:36:10.853
which I think can become very negative in the relationship.

01:36:10.853 --> 01:36:13.493
So letting go of the expectations of your partner

01:36:13.493 --> 01:36:17.253
is probably the key to a happier life in all sorts of ways.

01:36:17.253 --> 01:36:18.533
Did Richard do that?

01:36:18.533 --> 01:36:21.682
Yeah, he was very patient with me. (laughs)

01:36:21.682 --> 01:36:25.273
In fact, when at one point I think cause

01:36:25.273 --> 01:36:27.493
steak used to be my favorite meal and burgers.

01:36:27.493 --> 01:36:29.913
I'd always all order burgers when I was out and steak,

01:36:29.913 --> 01:36:30.963
I loved steak.

01:36:30.963 --> 01:36:32.933
And once he even cooked a steak for me,

01:36:32.933 --> 01:36:34.366
that shows much he loves me.

01:36:35.273 --> 01:36:37.563
Oh, it's strategic.

01:36:37.563 --> 01:36:38.893
Yeah, exactly.

01:36:38.893 --> 01:36:40.213
It's like reverse psychology.

01:36:40.213 --> 01:36:41.780
Right, so it was in the back.

01:36:41.780 --> 01:36:45.293
"Oh, if I do this." Right, who knows?

01:36:45.293 --> 01:36:47.323
But that brings up an important issue,

01:36:47.323 --> 01:36:49.923
which is somebody who's listening to this

01:36:49.923 --> 01:36:52.343
or watching it is thinking, well, my life is just gonna

01:36:52.343 --> 01:36:54.473
become infinitely more complicated.

01:36:54.473 --> 01:36:55.443
It means like we're gonna have

01:36:55.443 --> 01:36:57.183
to prepare two different meals.

01:36:57.183 --> 01:36:59.123
I'm gonna have to spend all this time in the kitchen.

01:36:59.123 --> 01:37:01.763
I'm gonna have to plan out everything in advance.

01:37:01.763 --> 01:37:05.193
It's probably gonna be expensive and suddenly my life,

01:37:05.193 --> 01:37:07.143
which was kind of going along pretty good,

01:37:07.143 --> 01:37:10.703
suddenly gets hijacked by this new lifestyle habit that is

01:37:10.703 --> 01:37:13.583
gonna commander all my time, energy and money.

01:37:13.583 --> 01:37:15.393
And is that the person that's wanting to start this?

01:37:15.393 --> 01:37:16.873
Or is this the partner of the person that's

01:37:16.873 --> 01:37:17.706
wanting to start this?

01:37:17.706 --> 01:37:19.443
It's the plant curious person

01:37:19.443 --> 01:37:21.213
who's contemplating the change,

01:37:21.213 --> 01:37:23.503
but now suddenly feels like maybe

01:37:23.503 --> 01:37:25.043
this is a little bit too difficult.

01:37:25.043 --> 01:37:27.853
So it's kind of moving from contemplation to action

01:37:27.853 --> 01:37:29.913
that people tend to have a stumbling block.

01:37:29.913 --> 01:37:31.433
Well, they're hearing you and they're like,

01:37:31.433 --> 01:37:32.913
my partner's not gonna be into this.

01:37:32.913 --> 01:37:34.280
So am I willing to

01:37:34.280 --> 01:37:35.653
Rock the boat?

01:37:35.653 --> 01:37:36.983
Yeah, do this thing

01:37:36.983 --> 01:37:41.113
that's gonna potentially cause fissures

01:37:41.113 --> 01:37:44.333
in my relationship and take up a lot of my time.

01:37:44.333 --> 01:37:47.213
Yeah, and those are valid, very valid concerns.

01:37:47.213 --> 01:37:50.453
And I guess what I'd say to that person is

01:37:50.453 --> 01:37:54.933
you have to find the thing that helps you know

01:37:54.933 --> 01:37:56.973
where you are going for the future.

01:37:56.973 --> 01:37:58.783
I think a lot of the time we kind of

01:37:59.738 --> 01:38:01.843
coast through and we do things

01:38:01.843 --> 01:38:05.293
because they are the path of least resistance.

01:38:05.293 --> 01:38:09.343
And sometimes the path of least resistance is a great path

01:38:09.343 --> 01:38:12.253
and it works and everything's going well.

01:38:12.253 --> 01:38:15.433
But sometimes over the course of maybe weeks,

01:38:15.433 --> 01:38:17.453
months to years, we start to notice actually,

01:38:17.453 --> 01:38:20.473
this isn't really working for me anymore.

01:38:20.473 --> 01:38:25.103
Although this is the easier route, I'm not feeling this.

01:38:25.103 --> 01:38:29.673
I either don't feel healthy or there's something niggling me

01:38:29.673 --> 01:38:33.103
in my conscience about things I wanna do differently.

01:38:33.103 --> 01:38:37.093
And sometimes changes do require

01:38:37.093 --> 01:38:39.133
a certain amount of forward thinking.

01:38:39.133 --> 01:38:41.563
And sometimes the biggest changes that we make in our lives

01:38:41.563 --> 01:38:44.343
are as a result of pain points,

01:38:44.343 --> 01:38:47.269
things that come up in our lives that we don't like.

01:38:47.269 --> 01:38:50.233
Hello.
(laughs)

01:38:50.233 --> 01:38:53.236
Exactly, pain can be our biggest teacher.

01:38:53.236 --> 01:38:58.043
And for those who are hoping to start making changes,

01:38:58.043 --> 01:38:59.983
but that pain point isn't quite there yet,

01:38:59.983 --> 01:39:01.563
then maybe they won't make a change.

01:39:01.563 --> 01:39:04.203
Maybe this won't be the right time for them.

01:39:04.203 --> 01:39:08.263
But my hope is that if we plant these seeds together today,

01:39:08.263 --> 01:39:11.813
some people will have enough water to plant

01:39:11.813 --> 01:39:13.363
to water those seeds.

01:39:13.363 --> 01:39:16.668
And other people may have enough sunlight to provide those

01:39:16.668 --> 01:39:18.453
seeds with a little bit of sunshine.

01:39:18.453 --> 01:39:20.883
And maybe those seeds will grow into plants of change.

01:39:20.883 --> 01:39:21.716
Based on today.

01:39:21.716 --> 01:39:24.493
So the person who's sticking that seed into the soil

01:39:24.493 --> 01:39:26.223
right now and getting ready to water it,

01:39:26.223 --> 01:39:27.483
but doesn't know how to water it.

01:39:27.483 --> 01:39:31.674
Like how do you think about the early phases of this?

01:39:31.674 --> 01:39:33.283
I'm somebody who went all in.

01:39:33.283 --> 01:39:35.573
That's just my personality type.

01:39:35.573 --> 01:39:38.553
I recognize most people aren't like that.

01:39:38.553 --> 01:39:41.373
I suspect you're somebody who would counsel people to make

01:39:41.373 --> 01:39:42.963
tiny changes, master them.

01:39:42.963 --> 01:39:46.063
Like how do you get somebody engaged in this

01:39:46.063 --> 01:39:48.643
and give them some actionable advice

01:39:48.643 --> 01:39:51.906
about how to begin in a very practical manner?

01:39:51.906 --> 01:39:55.243
So number one is literally write down the foods

01:39:55.243 --> 01:39:57.273
that you buy in your weekly shop.

01:39:57.273 --> 01:39:59.083
Number two is write down all the foods

01:39:59.083 --> 01:40:01.953
that you make with those ingredients.

01:40:01.953 --> 01:40:05.543
And if you can find one meal a week

01:40:05.543 --> 01:40:07.433
that you'd like to have more plants in,

01:40:07.433 --> 01:40:10.643
then that can be your first step is literally just cook one

01:40:10.643 --> 01:40:14.899
meal a week that is plant-based and see how you feel.

01:40:14.899 --> 01:40:16.883
And then if that works for you,

01:40:16.883 --> 01:40:18.435
then you can cook another one

01:40:18.435 --> 01:40:20.753
and then maybe cook another one.

01:40:20.753 --> 01:40:22.987
Yeah, but all I do is eat takeout.

01:40:22.987 --> 01:40:25.883
(laughs) Well, if you eat takeout then

01:40:25.883 --> 01:40:27.383
interestingly in the US,

01:40:27.383 --> 01:40:29.923
I dunno if it's the same, but in the UK

01:40:31.526 --> 01:40:35.623
we have a, pretty good plant-based ready meal range

01:40:35.623 --> 01:40:37.203
in a lot of our supermarkets.

01:40:37.203 --> 01:40:38.643
And interestingly, I had a patient

01:40:38.643 --> 01:40:42.693
who was elderly type two diabetic, never cooked for himself.

01:40:42.693 --> 01:40:44.193
And I said to him,

01:40:44.193 --> 01:40:46.743
why don't you just swap that one ready meal

01:40:46.743 --> 01:40:48.503
that you buy for this ready meal,

01:40:48.503 --> 01:40:52.243
which has all plants in it and in doing so he was able

01:40:52.243 --> 01:40:54.883
to come off insulin and improve his type two diabetes.

01:40:54.883 --> 01:40:57.803
So there's always a starting point for everybody.

01:40:57.803 --> 01:40:59.523
If you're somebody that just eats takeout,

01:40:59.523 --> 01:41:01.493
then you know what for you,

01:41:01.493 --> 01:41:03.793
the best thing would be perhaps just to start with one home

01:41:03.793 --> 01:41:06.133
cook meal a week, rather than trying to change everything,

01:41:06.133 --> 01:41:07.483
just cook one meal at home,

01:41:08.820 --> 01:41:10.453
rather than having all meals takeout.

01:41:10.453 --> 01:41:14.173
For my elderly patient who was just eating like

01:41:14.173 --> 01:41:16.393
small ready meals from the supermarket,

01:41:16.393 --> 01:41:18.933
switch to a plant-based one and see how you feel,

01:41:18.933 --> 01:41:21.053
see if it changes your health over time.

01:41:21.053 --> 01:41:21.886
So it's just,

01:41:21.886 --> 01:41:24.406
it is for a lot of people about those baby steps.

01:41:25.287 --> 01:41:28.533
For others, like you, they jump in and they'll get started

01:41:28.533 --> 01:41:29.773
and they'll figure it out along the way

01:41:29.773 --> 01:41:31.953
and they'll enjoy that process, and that's great.

01:41:31.953 --> 01:41:34.603
But for most people it's just little steps

01:41:34.603 --> 01:41:38.673
and there was research by BJ Fogg to show that only

01:41:38.673 --> 01:41:42.221
one change is enough to then start changing that,

01:41:42.221 --> 01:41:44.993
shifting that identity and then sort of adding

01:41:44.993 --> 01:41:46.123
in something new after that.

01:41:46.123 --> 01:41:47.683
So yeah just one thing,

01:41:47.683 --> 01:41:49.323
one thing is all you need to do.

01:41:49.323 --> 01:41:50.943
I'm getting BJ Fogg in here.

01:41:50.943 --> 01:41:53.983
I've been going back and forth with him on email forever.

01:41:53.983 --> 01:41:55.943
He's getting in here soon though.

01:41:55.943 --> 01:41:56.923
Yeah, I like that idea.

01:41:56.923 --> 01:42:00.683
I think in listening to you, I'm reflecting on,

01:42:00.683 --> 01:42:04.623
some of my habits now and what I find myself doing from

01:42:04.623 --> 01:42:07.873
time to time when I'm busy or Julie's outta town

01:42:07.873 --> 01:42:09.793
or she's I'm not cooking tonight.

01:42:09.793 --> 01:42:11.753
I go get plant-based takeout,

01:42:11.753 --> 01:42:13.773
but you have to be really careful

01:42:13.773 --> 01:42:15.443
because when you're getting takeout food,

01:42:15.443 --> 01:42:16.613
you're not preparing the food,

01:42:16.613 --> 01:42:19.493
there's gonna be more oil in it than you think,

01:42:19.493 --> 01:42:22.153
there's gonna be more salt in it than you think.

01:42:22.153 --> 01:42:26.443
And if you're opting for some of these meat and dairy

01:42:26.443 --> 01:42:30.333
analogs, like maybe not so good, not all of 'them

01:42:30.333 --> 01:42:32.546
they're all, it depends on what.

01:42:33.626 --> 01:42:34.542
It does.

01:42:34.542 --> 01:42:36.393
Because there's a spectrum, and in terms of health

01:42:36.393 --> 01:42:40.036
the health factors that are what these things are about,

01:42:40.943 --> 01:42:44.903
but how do you think about the Beyond Burgers

01:42:44.903 --> 01:42:48.013
and the Impossible Burgers and all of that kind of stuff.

01:42:48.013 --> 01:42:50.453
I like to be fairly practical in the advice

01:42:50.453 --> 01:42:51.453
I give to people.

01:42:51.453 --> 01:42:54.063
I think if somebody likes the taste of an Impossible

01:42:54.063 --> 01:42:56.593
or Beyond Burger and wants to have it once or twice a week,

01:42:56.593 --> 01:42:57.943
then there's no reason why they shouldn't

01:42:57.943 --> 01:42:59.703
for the most part, especially early-

01:42:59.703 --> 01:43:00.543
Exactly.

01:43:00.543 --> 01:43:02.195
In the transition.

01:43:02.195 --> 01:43:03.073
But then having said that,

01:43:03.073 --> 01:43:05.213
there is interesting data emerging

01:43:05.213 --> 01:43:08.063
that there was a study that one of my friends did actually

01:43:08.063 --> 01:43:11.383
on a specific brand of plant-based

01:43:11.383 --> 01:43:14.869
meat replacement, meatballs and mince.

01:43:14.869 --> 01:43:16.793
And in the study,

01:43:16.793 --> 01:43:21.663
families were given these meat replacements to use alongside

01:43:21.663 --> 01:43:24.613
the normal meat and other foods that they would prepare.

01:43:24.613 --> 01:43:26.493
And what they found is that in the families

01:43:26.493 --> 01:43:27.713
that had the meat replacement,

01:43:27.713 --> 01:43:30.363
they had a favorable change in their microbiome

01:43:30.363 --> 01:43:32.643
over the course of those.

01:43:32.643 --> 01:43:34.263
I don't remember how many weeks the study was,

01:43:34.263 --> 01:43:37.143
but it was perhaps about four weeks to my recollection.

01:43:37.143 --> 01:43:40.543
They had really great shifts in their microbial health

01:43:40.543 --> 01:43:43.863
from using meat replacement analogs.

01:43:43.863 --> 01:43:48.153
So I think we do need more research to understand the effect

01:43:48.153 --> 01:43:50.393
on the body, but we do also know

01:43:50.393 --> 01:43:53.463
for in the majority of these meat replacement analogs,

01:43:53.463 --> 01:43:56.373
there will be less saturated fat.

01:43:56.373 --> 01:43:58.843
There will be less or no heme iron,

01:43:58.843 --> 01:44:00.693
which are both potential issues

01:44:00.693 --> 01:44:01.833
when it comes to heart disease,

01:44:01.833 --> 01:44:03.953
risk and inflammation in the body.

01:44:03.953 --> 01:44:07.883
And I think they are too easily demonized

01:44:07.883 --> 01:44:08.906
by a lot of people.

01:44:09.953 --> 01:44:11.813
And yeah, that's my take on it.

01:44:11.813 --> 01:44:13.733
I wouldn't say you should have them every day.

01:44:13.733 --> 01:44:15.373
But it's the same with any kind of food.

01:44:15.373 --> 01:44:17.363
It's great to have variety and whole foods

01:44:17.363 --> 01:44:19.383
as your main go-to.

01:44:19.383 --> 01:44:22.162
But I think a lot of people are too quick

01:44:22.162 --> 01:44:24.523
to cast off these meat replacements.

01:44:24.523 --> 01:44:25.373
Right, yeah.

01:44:25.373 --> 01:44:29.003
Well, again, it's a spectrum, some are worse than others.

01:44:29.003 --> 01:44:31.623
And there's a difference between health promoting

01:44:31.623 --> 01:44:34.563
and neutral versus deleterious to your health.

01:44:34.563 --> 01:44:35.396
Exactly.

01:44:35.396 --> 01:44:40.103
I think what is becoming a bigger issue over time

01:44:42.331 --> 01:44:45.013
is that now there are so many products out there

01:44:45.013 --> 01:44:46.343
that taste really good

01:44:46.343 --> 01:44:49.943
and you can kind of psychologically rationalize

01:44:49.943 --> 01:44:52.763
eating them like, oh, it's vegan, it's plant-based.

01:44:52.763 --> 01:44:54.919
Like it's gotta be good for me, right?

01:44:54.919 --> 01:44:57.113
And wake up only to realize like,

01:44:57.113 --> 01:44:59.863
you're eating French fries and Beyond Burgers all the time.

01:44:59.863 --> 01:45:02.444
That's not a whole food plant-based diet.

01:45:02.444 --> 01:45:05.763
And it becomes incumbent upon you to like really make sure

01:45:05.763 --> 01:45:08.903
that you're eating plant foods close to their natural state

01:45:08.903 --> 01:45:11.093
as wide a diversity as possible

01:45:11.093 --> 01:45:12.923
rather than diluting yourself

01:45:12.923 --> 01:45:15.913
that because you're not eating cheeseburgers,

01:45:15.913 --> 01:45:18.421
you're eating a plant version of that,

01:45:18.421 --> 01:45:21.155
so you're gonna sidestep heart disease, right.

01:45:21.155 --> 01:45:22.360
Yeah, exactly.

01:45:22.360 --> 01:45:23.593
It's hard.

01:45:23.593 --> 01:45:25.863
It's kind of like the stuff finally tastes good

01:45:25.863 --> 01:45:26.696
for the most part

01:45:26.696 --> 01:45:30.224
and it's gonna continue to get better and better and better.

01:45:30.224 --> 01:45:32.223
I think what we're gonna see is like a split.

01:45:32.223 --> 01:45:33.393
We're gonna have foods

01:45:33.393 --> 01:45:36.393
that are gonna taste exactly like that thing,

01:45:36.393 --> 01:45:39.873
but they're gonna be probably on the more unhealthy side.

01:45:39.873 --> 01:45:42.903
And then there's gonna be other companies who are creating

01:45:42.903 --> 01:45:45.713
plant analogs to some of our favorite foods

01:45:45.713 --> 01:45:47.563
that are doing it with health in mind

01:45:48.511 --> 01:45:50.573
and lesser ingredients and natural ingredients.

01:45:50.573 --> 01:45:53.713
And my hope is that there's a lot of focus and energy

01:45:53.713 --> 01:45:55.503
and money going into that right now.

01:45:55.503 --> 01:45:57.153
Cause I think that's what we need more of.

01:45:57.153 --> 01:45:57.986
I agree with you.

01:45:57.986 --> 01:45:59.733
I think that you are absolutely right.

01:45:59.733 --> 01:46:03.143
We want minimally processed, less ingredients,

01:46:03.143 --> 01:46:06.943
more whole food based analogs for sure.

01:46:06.943 --> 01:46:09.633
And the ingredients do vary really widely.

01:46:09.633 --> 01:46:12.993
So yeah, I think when you look at the data

01:46:12.993 --> 01:46:16.643
on plant-based diets and the healthy plant-based diet index,

01:46:16.643 --> 01:46:19.968
which was really interesting data, you see,

01:46:19.968 --> 01:46:24.543
as you've pointed out that if you're having an unhealthy

01:46:24.543 --> 01:46:26.790
plant-based diet with things like

01:46:26.790 --> 01:46:31.273
French fries and ice cream and all the things that you can.

01:46:31.273 --> 01:46:34.017
Coconut ice cream and Oreos and potato chips.

01:46:34.017 --> 01:46:37.302
Oreos, crisps, potato chips, all of that kind of stuff.

01:46:37.302 --> 01:46:39.723
you're not gonna reap the same benefits at all.

01:46:39.723 --> 01:46:41.555
And you're right, people can delude themselves

01:46:41.555 --> 01:46:43.113
to think, oh, well it's healthy cause it's plant-based.

01:46:43.113 --> 01:46:44.963
Well it's not always.

01:46:44.963 --> 01:46:47.873
